T1  - 181. Is patient geography a risk factor for chronic opioid use after ACDF?
JF  - Spine Journal
A1  - Levy Hannah
A1  - Karamian Brian
A1  - Henstenburg Jeffrey
A1  - Canseco Jose A
A1  - Larwa Joseph
A1  - Haislup Brett
A1  - Hilibrand Alan S
A1  - Kepler Christopher K
A1  - Vaccaro Alexander R
A1  - Schroeder Gregory D
KW  - eppi-reviewer
KW  - PHARMACEUTICAL services insurance
KW  - PREOPERATIVE risk factors
KW  - OPIOIDS
KW  - ZIP codes
KW  - OPIOID abuse
KW  - PENNSYLVANIA
AB  - Opioid-related mortality disproportionately affects socioeconomically disadvantaged populations suffering from increased physical and mental health conditions, polysubstance abuse, decreased social supports, barriers to education, and housing and job insecurity. The social and medical implications intrinsic to patient zip codes with high opioid fatality may reveal residence in these locations to be a risk factor predicting chronic opioid use after anterior cervical discectomy and fusion (ACDF). To determine if residence in Pennsylvania zip codes with high incidence of opioid overdose deaths is a risk factor for chronic postoperative opioid use after ACDF. Retrospective cohort analysis. All patients over age 18 who underwent 1- to 4-level primary or revision ACDF for cervical spondylosis associated with radiculopathy and/or myelopathy at a high-volume academic center between 2016-2019 were retrospectively identified. Exclusion criteria included absence of descriptive data or opioid utilization records, or surgery performed for trauma, infection, or malignancy. Postoperative opioid use prescription metrics: duration of usage and the number of prescriptions and pharmacies utilized. Preoperative opioid and benzodiazepine tolerance. Chronicity of postoperative opioid use. Patients undergoing ACDF for degenerative pathologies at an academic center were identified. According to the recommendations of the Overdose Free PA initiative, patients were divided into binary groups based on high (>5) and low (≤5) number of opioid overdose deaths per residential zip code. Pre- and postsurgical opioid and benzodiazepine use were obtained from the Pennsylvania Prescription Drug Monitoring Program (PDMP). Postoperative opioid chronicity (≥120 days or >10 prescriptions) and time to opioid discontinuation were compared across zip code groups with univariate analysis and Kaplan Meier curves, respectively. Binary logistic regression isolated predictors of prolonged opioid use. A total of 298 patients met the inclusion criteria. Preoperative opioid usage did not vary meaningfully between high- and low-risk zip code groups. Patients living in high-risk opioid overdose zip codes were significantly more likely to exhibit chronic opioid use patterns after ACDF. Kaplan Meier curve demonstrated that opioid discontinuation was less probable at any postoperative time for patients residing in zip codes associated with high opioid fatality. Logistic regression isolated preoperative risk factors of opioid tolerance, current smoking status, and depression diagnosis as predictors of extended opioid use (>1 postoperative prescription). Patient residence in a zip code with a high incidence of opioid overdose deaths was shown to correlate with long-term continuation and chronic usage of opioid medication after ACDF. Further investigation of the sociocultural and medical disparities inherent to these high-risk zip codes is required to direct community interventions addressing prescription opioid misuse. This abstract does not discuss or include any applicable devices or drugs. [ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR] Copyright of Spine Journal is the property of Elsevier B.V. and its content may not be copied or emailed to multiple sites or posted to a listserv without the copyright holder's express written permission. However, users may print, download, or email articles for individual use. This abstract may be abridged. No warranty is given about the accuracy of the copy. Users should refer to the original published version of the material for the full abstract. (Copyright applies to all Abstracts.)
