AB  - Additional examination (see Part 1, 1998 of the 322 homeless & 642 home-based clients of the Community Psychiatric Nursing service (CPNs) in Bloomsbury, a district of London, England, compared the referral process & the CPNs intervention in the two groups. The homeless clients had different referral patterns, received different types of care, & received few hours of care, even when controlling for the type of problems they presented. This difference appears to be due to the multiplicity & severity of homeless clients' problems, which the recording system was not designed to designate. Also, most of the homeless clients were inappropriately referred by informal services, & the CPNs then had to refer them to another service. These findings show that homeless people need multidisciplinary mental health services to meet their complex needs. 6 Tables, 21 References. Adapted from the source document.
