AB  - Examines ways to decrease risk factors for eating disorders among female college athletes, drawing on pre- & posttest data from 70 athletes in seven major university sports who were randomly assigned to either an 8-week health education intervention or a comparison group. Findings indicate that intervention subjects scored lower on drive for thinness & body dissatisfaction while the comparison group was lower on self-esteem & nutrition knowledge. Regression analyses revealed that the educational intervention was associated with decreased drive for thinness, & decreased drive for thinness was associated with decreased body dissatisfaction. It is concluded that the intervention shows promise for helping female collegiate athletes at high-risk for eating disorders. 3 Tables, 1 Figure, 44 References. Adapted from the source document.
