AB  - Interdisciplinary care, such as that provided in chronic pain rehabilitation programs (CPRPS), is the gold standard for treating chronic pelvic pain (CPP). Patients treated in CPRPs typically demonstrate robust improvements in pain, mood and physical function. One common critique is that treatment outcomes are based on self-report and thus subjective. This study addresses this critique through investigation of subjective and objective outcomes in women with CPP undergoing treatment in a CPRP. Participants included 58 patients with CPP matched by age and treatment date to 58 patients with non-pelvic chronic pain (NPCP). The majority were white (88.79%), married (54.31%), and had multiple pain concerns (92.2%). Participants mean age was 42.74 (+/-12.82) with a pain duration of 12.75 (+/-12.82). Treatment was an intensive, outpatient, 3-4-week program including medication management, weaning from habituating medications, physical/occupational therapy and individual, group and family psychotherapy. Outcomes were collected at admission and discharge. Subjective measurements included pain severity (Numeric Pain Rating Scale - 11), depression and anxiety (Depression, Anxiety and Stress Scale), functional impairment (Pain Disability Index) and catastrophizing (Pain Catastrophizing Scale). Objective measurements of physical function included: timed up and go, 6-minute walking, and stair climbing. Linear mixed models, with fixed factors of group, time, and their interaction, were constructed. Across all measures, improvements across time were significant (p = .00). There were no group differences, except in depression, which showed women with CPP reported greater improvements (p = .05) vs. NPCP, with higher depression scores at admission and no significant difference at discharge. Post hoc ANOVA showed this difference was due to higher levels of baseline depression in women with CPP (p = .03). Results demonstrate that women with CPP attain treatment benefits equivalent to that of their peers and report normalization of mood post treatment, even with higher baseline levels of depression.
