AB  - 62 Hispanic, 51 Black, and 60 White psychiatric outpatients in a large public psychiatric clinic participated in an orientation program prior to their 1st psychotherapy interview. The group was 47% male (mean age 31 yrs) and 53% female (mean age 34 yrs). Ss were presented with 1 of 2 experimental conditions; the oriented Ss saw a 12-min audiovisual program that instructed them about psychotherapy, and the controls saw a program that was neutral with regard to psychotherapy. Ss' knowledge and attitudes toward psychotherapy were assessed with 2 questionnaires. Results indicate that Ss who were oriented were more knowledgeable about psychotherapy and more positive in their attitudes toward psychotherapy than were Ss who had not been oriented. The role and utility of brief orientation programs for low-income and ethnic minority outpatients in public mental health facilities are discussed. (21 ref) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
