AB  - INTERVENTION: The intervention will include supervised, small group‐based (1‐4 participants) aerobic and resistance exercise training three times per week over a 12 week period. Exercise sessions will be led by exercise physiologists who have experience working with individuals with cancer and will be delivered in community fitness centers. Continuous aerobic exercise will be performed on a stationary exercise bike or treadmill for 10‐20 minutes at 55‐65% estimated heart rate maximum (HRmax), or a Borg rating of perceived exertion (RPE) of 12‐13 out of 20, for the first two weeks and will progress to moderate intensity interval training at 70‐80% HRmax, or an RPE of 15‐16. High intensity interval training sessions at 85‐95% HRmax, or RPE of 18‐19, will be introduced in week four. Resistance exercise will include up to six exercises to target major upper and lower body muscle groups (e.g. leg press, chest press, seated row) using machines, free weights or body weight. Resistance exercise will start at two sets at 10‐12 repetition maximum (RM), progressing to 3‐4 sets of 6‐10 RM by week nine. Exercise will be modified and progressed according to individual tolerability. Compliance and attendance (including the reasons for missed sessions or inability to meet exercise targets) will be tracked throughout the intervention by the supervising exercise physiologist. A 30 g serving of whey protein isolate powder (26 g of protein) mixed with 250‐300 mL of water will be provided to all participants following each exercise session. Exercise physiologists will administer the protein supplement and track adherence. Subgroup analyses will be performed on a select number of intervention and usual care participants who choose to opt‐in to additional assessments upon enrollment in the trial. CONDITION: Cancer ‐ Any cancer Cancer ;Cachexia; ; Cancer ; Cachexia Musculoskeletal ‐ Other muscular and skeletal disorders PRIMARY OUTCOME: Feasibility of exercise session frequency (i.e. attendance), adherence to supervised aerobic and resistance exercise, tolerance of intervention and incidence/severity of any adverse events (e.g. muscle strain as a result of exercise) as measured by review of exercise session registers kept by the exercise physiologist.[At the end of the 12 week intervention] Feasibility of participant recruitment (i.e. attrition) and retention, as measured as the proportion of patients screened and eligible versus those who consented and enrolled and the number of participants who do not request withdrawal from the study, respectively. [At the end of the 12 week intervention] Participant exercise program satisfaction measured using a researcher‐generated questionnaire.[At the end of the 12 week intervention] SECONDARY OUTCOME: Anxiety and depression will be evaluated using the Hospital Anxiety and Depression Scale (HADS).[At baseline, 6 weeks, 12 weeks and 24 weeks after randomisation] Cancer‐related fatigue will be evaluated using the Functional Assessment of Chronic Illness Therapy‐Fatigue (FACIT‐F).[At baseline, 6 weeks, 12 weeks and 24 weeks after randomisation] Dietary intake will be evaluated using a three‐day food diary. [At baseline, 12 weeks and 24 weeks after randomisation] Exercise motivation and beliefs will be evaluated using a researcher‐generated questionnaire based on the theory of planned behaviour. ; ; [At baseline, 6 weeks, 12 weeks and 24 weeks after randomisation] Gait speed will be evaluated using the six minute walk test.[At baseline, 12 weeks and 24 weeks after randomisation] General patient‐reported quality of life will be evaluated using the Medical Outcomes Short Form (SF‐36) questionnaire. [At baseline, 6 weeks, 12 weeks and 24 weeks after randomisation] Lean body mass and fat mass will be evaluated using dual‐energy X‐ray absorptiometry scans.[At baseline, 12 weeks and 24 weeks after randomisation] Maximal aerobic capacity will be estimated using a submaximal incremental aerobic exercise test on a cycle ergometer.[At baseline, 12 weeks and 24 weeks after randomisation] Muscular strength will be evaluated using tests for maximal leg press 1‐repetition maximum, maximal hand grip strength and the 30 second chair rise. [At baseline, 12 weeks and 24 weeks after randomisation] Nutritional status will be evaluated using the Patient‐Generated Subjective Global Assessment (PG‐SGA).[At baseline, 12 weeks and 24 weeks after randomisation] One‐on‐one interviews (approx. 30 minutes) to explore key themes associated with the acceptability of exercise and the perceived impact of the intervention on well‐being will be conducted by an experienced interviewer, not directly involved in delivering the intervention. All interviews will be audio recorded. ; ; [At the end of the 12 week intervention. ; ; ] Patient‐reported cancer‐specific quality of life and cachexia/anorexia symptoms will be evaluated using the Functional Assessment Anorexia/Cachexia Treatment (FAACT) questionnaire.[At baseline, 6 weeks, 12 weeks and 24 weeks after randomisation] Physical activity and free‐living energy expenditure will be evaluated using accelerometers.[At baseline, 12 weeks and 24 weeks after randomisation] Resting metabolic rate will be evaluated via indirect calorimetry in a subgroup of participants who opt‐in to complete these assessments upon enrollment. ; ; [At baseline, 12 weeks and 24 weeks after randomisation] Self‐reported physical activity levels will be evaluated using a modified Godin Leisure Time Exercise questionnaire. [At baseline, 6 weeks, 12 weeks and 24 weeks after randomisation] INCLUSION CRITERIA: 1. Confirmed diagnosis of advanced cancer (i.e. locally advanced or metastatic disease) 2. Body mass index (BMI) <30 mg/m2 3. Australian‐modified Karnofsky Performance Status >70 4. Weight loss >5% within the past 6 months, or BMI <20 kg/m2 and weight loss >2% within the past 6 months, or presence of sarcopenia and weight loss >2% within the past 6 months
