AB  - Introduction The sensory innervation to the pelvic portion of the hip joint is complex, with contributions from the femoral, obturator, and sciatic nerves (Birnbaum et al., 1997). Fractures of the acetabulum thus present a challenge when controlling pain with a regional anesthetic technique. We present a case highlighting the successful use of a fascia iliaca compartment block and continuous nerve catheter for pain control in a patient with an acetabular fracture. Results/Case report The patient was a 75 year old man with a history of dementia, NASH cirrhosis complicated by hepatic encephalopathy and elevated INR, coronary artery disease, and severe aortic stenosis. He presented with a left acetabular fracture with mild displacement of both columns after a ground level fall. Given his extensive comorbidities he was deemed a non-operative candidate with a recommendation for immediate weight bearing and gait training. Attempts at controlling his pain with a multimodal medical regimen had failed and the regional anesthesia service was consulted. A fascia iliaca compartment block was performed with 60 cc of 0.25% bupivacaine with 1:400,000 epinephrine using ultrasound guidance. In addition to the block, a continuous nerve catheter was placed and an infusion of 7 cc/hr of 0.2% bupivacaine was started. Following the block and catheter placement the patient experienced an immediate improvement in mobility, tolerating 90 degrees of passive range of motion as well as transitioning to a seated position for the first time since admission. The catheter was continued for three days before being removed. The patient experienced progressive improvement in mobility and was discharged to an acute rehabilitation center. Discussion Poorly controlled hip fracture pain is a major healthcare concern, leading to increased hospital length of stay and long-term functional impairment (Morrison et al., 2003). Achieving adequate pain control in this case proved to be particularly challenging for two reasons. First, the patient's existing dementia and hepatic encephalopathy made opioids and adjuvants such as acetaminophen unappealing options. Second, the patient's elevated INR precluded the use of any neuraxial form of pain control. A regional anesthetic approach was therefore felt to be the safest option. A fascia iliaca compartment block is designed to cover the femoral, obturator, and lateral femoral cutaneous nerves, two of which supply sensation to a portion of the acetabulum. We found that published evidence for the use of a fascia iliaca block for acetabular pain is largely limited to pelvic osteotomies in the pediatric population (Lako et al., 2009 and Miller, 2011). We believe this is one of the few reported cases demonstrating the use of a fascia iliaca catheter and continuous infusion for the treatment of acetabular fracture pain in adults and hope it serves as a positive example.
