AB  - Introduction: The combination of emotional and somatic symptoms such as pain and depression can have an enormous negative impact on the quality of life of patients. There are few data making psychiatrists aware of the need to approach pain as part of depression assessment, although depression and pain share biological pathways and neurotransmitters, which has implications for the treatment of both concurrently. Also, the presence of pain negatively affects the recognition and treatment of depression [1]. Purpose of the study: To identify the prevalence of pain of unknown aetiology or of known aetiology but disproportionate severity, in a sample of patients with depression who received psychiatric care and to evaluate possible differences in subgroups defined by age, gender and presence of pain. Secondary outcome measures evaluated the relationship between intensity and location of pain and intensity of depression. Method(s): 3,566 patients aged 18 or older visiting a psychiatrist for the first time and receiving a DSM-IV-TR diagnosis of depression were studied. Severity of depression was evaluated by the HAM-D rating scale. Type and severity of pain, if any, was assessed by Visual Analogic Scales (VAS). Patients were asked about interference of the pain with quantity and quality of sleep. Analgesic and psychiatric drug treatments taken by the patients were also recorded. Summary of results: Mean age was 49.43 (SD: 13.02) years. Gender: 71% female. Major depression (72%) and dysthimia (17%) were the most frequent diagnoses. The time elapsed since the first pain symptom until the diagnosis of depression was 48.35 months. The prevalence of pain in patients with depression was 59.1%, with a statistically significant increase related to age and female gender (63% vs 47%). Pain severity also increased with age. Prevalence of pain in patients with dysthimia (68.6%) was higher than in patients with depresion (55.4%). In patients with pain and depression, prevalence of depressive symptoms such as depressive mood, anhedonia, diminished ability to think or concentrate, sleep disorders, changes in weight/appetite, feelings of worthlessness or excessive guilt, psychomotor agitation or retardation, suicidal ideation was higher. The score on the Hamilton rating scale was higher in patients with pain (24.87 vs 22.48) both in the adult and elderly populations. No differences were found depending on pain location except for joint pain. Analgesic and psychotropic drug use was higher in elderly patients. Conclusion(s): The high prevalence of pain in patients with depression highlights the importance of identifying painful symptoms in patients with this diagnosis, something about physicians and psychiatrist don't always ask. This has many implications because there are treatments such as tricyclic antidepressants which are more effective in treating painful physical symptoms and bearing this in mind will give us a more global approach to this prevalent disorder. For instance, newer generation dual acting antidepressants such as duloxetine and venlafaxine offer a welltolerated and safe alternative to tricyclics [2].
