AB  - Excess morbidity and mortality among African Americans is associated with smoking-related disease. Smoking prevalence among black women in Ohio was 43%. This study (1) described sociodemographic and smoking history characteristics of 187 black women and (2) examined a model of nicotine dependence among black women cigarette smokers. The model, derived from cognitive social learning theory, included variables of positive outcome expectancies, smoking to cope, general coping skills, and nicotine intake. Nicotine dependence, smoking to cope, general coping skills and positive outcome expectancies were measured via self-report questionnaires among 187 black women smokers. Nicotine intake was assessed by salivary cotinine. Assays were completed using high performance liquid chromatography. A third purpose was to determine feasibility of conducting a 4-week mailed smoking cessation intervention with weekly support by a black woman exsmoker. In a subset, an experimental 3-group design with 22 women in each group was used to evaluate smoking cessation intervention feasibility and successful quitting at 6 and 12 weeks after women entered the study. Three groups were: intervention, one-time advice, and waiting list control. Age range for the 187 participants was 18-69 (M = 36); 59% reported <= 12 years of education; 40% were unemployed and 60% reported annual household income < $15,000; average length of regular smoking was 14 years with a mean of 15 cigarettes/day; 90 % smoked menthol cigarettes and 3 brands accounted for 67 % of those reported. Mean cotinine value was 402 ng/mL (range = 25--1052). 58 % reported <= 2 serious attempts to quit smoking and half expressed low levels of interest in quitting. Model variables explained 46 % of variance in nicotine dependence with smoking to cope, log transformed cigarettes/day, and positive outcome expectancies the best predictors ( it p / = .001). At 12 week follow-up, while there were no nonsmokers in the three groups, average cigarettes/day and cotinine had decreased. Limited success of intervention may be attributed to random assignment to treatment regardless of interest in quitting, few previous attempts to quit indicating many were in precontemplation stage, and inadequate testing of the lay facilitator concept. Interventions cannot assume readiness to quit smoking. Recommendations are that treatment components include development of broader coping skills, cognitive restructuring of expectancies about smoking, and incorporation of several lay facilitators to enhance support.
