AB  - Described the main principles of a psychotherapeutically oriented approach to schizophrenia. Therapeutic activities are planned and executed individually to meet the changing needs of patients and their families. Examination and treatment are dominated by a psychotherapeutic attitude. The therapeutic activities should support and not impair each other. In addition, the process quality of therapy is clearly perceived. The prognosis of the patients, as evaluated in a follow-up survey conducted 2 yrs after admission, was compared with a series of patients of the schizophrenia group first admitted from the same catchment area in 1976–1977. Differences concerning the disappearance of manifest psychotic symptoms, avoidance of disability pension, and number of inpatient days per patient are discussed. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
