AB  - Background: Outpatient treatment for depression generally includes prescription medications (antidepressants) and psychotherapy/counselling, either alone or in combination. One challenge in mental health services is determining which treatment approaches work for who. How individuals rate their subjective experiences with treatment might indicate the effectiveness of each treatment approach for depression, and might determine future engagement with treatment. Objective(s): To identify factors associated with receiving specific depression treatments, and to determine whether perceived outcomes of treatment vary by treatment type. Method(s): Data were obtained from the 2015 and 2016 samples of the National Survey on Drug Use and Health (NSDUH). We restricted our analyses to adults who received treatment for major depressive disorder in the past year. We used multinomial logistic regression to identify factors associated with the relative odds of receiving each type of treatment - medication only, counselling only, and counselling and medication. We also computed odds of rating treatment as effective for each treatment type while controlling for socio-economic and health characteristics of respondents including severity of symptoms and the pathway into care. Result(s): Persons living in small metropolitan areas were less likely to receive counselling relative to medication. Those who were coerced into care were also more likely to receive counselling than medication. Severity of symptoms was associated with receiving both medication and counselling compared to receiving medication or counselling only. There were no differences in perceived effectiveness of treatment between recipients of counselling services and those who received medication. However, persons who received both medication and counselling had significantly greater odds (OR=6.4, p=0.017) of rating treatment as effective compared to those who received medication alone. Among persons who received medication, African Americans were less likely than whites to rate medications as effective and persons who live in rural areas were significantly more likely to rate treatment as effective compared to their peers in large urban areas. Severity of symptoms was associated with lower odds of perceived effectiveness of treatment. The pathway into care was associated with perceived treatment effectiveness among persons who received counselling only. Being ordered into care was associated with lower odds of perceived effectiveness of counselling. Having poor self-assessed general health and being unemployed were associated with greater odds of rating counselling as effective. Among those who received both medication and counselling, health insurance was associated with greater odds of rating treatment as effective and the more severe the symptoms, the less likely respondents were to rate treatment as effective. Implications: This study adds to the body of research demonstrating that combined treatment for depression may be more beneficial than single-modality treatment. However, not everyone with depression might be able to access both medication and counselling. There may be benefits to increasing access to both forms of treatment for persons who are less likely to rate either single modality as effective. This includes people who are uninsured, ordered into care, and persons with severe symptoms.
