AB  - Common mental illnesses in old age, such as depressed mood and anxiety, co-occur with poor physical function and comorbid medical conditions, and together produce disability in daily activities. Yet most interventions to prevent disability target only one precursor. Most intervention research seeks to establish the specificity of an intervention effect, for example, a mental health intervention affecting only a mental health outcome, which is often taken as a sign of an appropriate underlying theory or proper engagement with a biologic target. The Positive Minds-Strong Bodies (PMSB) randomized controlled trial (M. Alegría et al, 2019) suggests that it may be time to rethink this approach. The PMSB trial assessed a combined intervention for mood symptoms (up to 10 sessions of cognitive behavioral therapy [CBT]) and lower extremity impairment (up to 36 exercise sessions) over 6 months to prevent disability at 6 and 12 months. Participants were aged more than 60 and scored 5 or more on either the Patient Health Questionnaire, Generalized Anxiety Disorder Scale, or Geriatric Depression Scale. At 6 months, participants in the intervention arm saw improvements in SPPB, self-reported disability (Late Life Functional Disability Instrument, LLFDI), and mood (Hopkins Symptom Checklist-25). No differences were evident in anxiety (GAD) or WHO Disability Assessment Schedule (WHODAS 2.0). At 12 months, participants in the intervention arm continued to report less disability (LLFDI, WHODAS) and improved mood (Hopkins Symptom Checklist). The PMSB intervention shows that a conjoint intervention to address both mental and physical health can lower the burden of disability. It recognizes that disability can emerge from either source and accordingly suggests we consider intervening in multiple pathways if we wish to lower the risk of disability. However, it is important to recognize as well what the study does not tell us. It is unclear, for example, how much of the reduction in disability components is due to better problem-solving or from exercise. Alegría et al remind us that by 2040 one third of older adults in the United States will belong to racial or ethnic minority groups. Disability remains the central public health challenge of aging, and mood disorders continue to be an important risk for earlier and more severe disability. PMSB is an important model for moving a promising preventive intervention into increasingly diverse aging communities and for stressing the need to focus on multiple, remediable sources of disability. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
