AB  - Objective: Analysis of Neurosurgical patients acutely discharged (AD) vs non-acutely discharged (NAD) from an Inpatient Rehabilitation Facility (IRF). Design(s): Retrospective chart review. Setting(s): Inpatient rehabilitation facility of an inner city hospital. Participant(s): 51 Neurosurgical patients admitted to an IRF. 31 females and 20 males. Intervention(s): Retrospective chart review of acute vs non acute discharges of neurosurgical patients admitted to an IRF. Main Outcome Measure(s): a) Age b) BMI c) Gender d) Average #postop days to IRF e) average length of stay in IRF f) Type of surgery g) Total FIM score h) Total FIM cognition score i) Total FIM motor score j) comorbidities, k) surgical diagnoses, l) reasons for AD. Result(s): Of the 51 patients studied, 9 (17.6%) were acutely discharged and 42 (82.35%) were NAD. AD patients were older with an average age of 65.33 years SD 10.23 vs 58.69 years SD 13.92, had a higher BMI 32.79 points SD 9.34 vs 29.64 points SD 7.00. Additionally, cranial surgeries had the longest time delay (7.33 days) from post op to admission to rehab, and among the AD patients, cranial surgeries were most prevalent 4 (44%). Most 50/51 patients had higher FIM scores on discharge 86.73 SD 24.32 vs admission 63.14 SD 18.36; however, AD patients had lower FIM scores on discharge 63.95 SD 24.32 vs NAD 87.83 SD 25.60. The average post-op day to rehab was 5.38 days in NAD patients and 5.11 days in AD patients. The average length of stay was 15.52 days in NAD patients and 6.33 days in AD patients. Reasons for AD included Altered Mental Status (AMS) (left frontal hematoma, cerebral edema) (2), post op ileus (1), lethargy (1), CSF leak (1), respiratory failure (1), serous discharge from wound (1), GI bleed (1), epilepsy (1). Among the AD patients, the comorbidity that was most commonly associated was hypertension (8/9). This was followed by Pulmonary Disease and Cancer (Lung, Prostate, Meningioma, s/p Brain Neoplasm Resection) (4/9); Diabetes Mellitus, Hemorrhage and Hypercholesterolemia (3/9); Spinal Cord Compressions (2/9); Seizures, Heart disease, Rheumatologic disease, and Degenerative Joint Disease (1/9). From acutely discharged patients 33% had a hemorrhagic complication resulting in neurosurgical intervention and 50% of those patients had AMS resulting in patient's acute discharge. Conclusion(s): Neurosurgical patients who were acutely discharged from IRF were older, had a higher BMI, and demonstrated lower functional outcomes. AMS was the most common cause of acute discharges from IRF in neurosurgical patients.
