AB  - Examines (1) the extent to which managed care participation is associated with technical efficiency in outpatient substance abuse treatment (OSAT) organizations & (2) the contributions of specific managed care practices as well as other organizational, financial, & environmental attributes to technical efficiency in these organizations, using 1995 data from a nationally representative sample survey of 474 OSAT organizations. Technical efficiency is modeled using data envelopment analysis. Overall, there were few significant associations between managed care dimensions & technical efficiency in outpatient treatment organizations. Only one managed care oversight procedure, the imposition of sanctions by managed care firms, was significantly associated with relative efficiency of these provider organizations. However, several organizational factors were associated with the relative level of efficiency, including hospital affiliation, mental health center affiliation, Joint Commission on the Accreditation of Health Care Organizations (JCAHO) accreditation, receipt of lump sum revenues, methadone treatment modality, % of clients unemployed, & % of clients who abuse multiple drugs. 5 Tables, 1 Appendix, 52 References. Adapted from the source document.
