AB  - Introduction Individual asthma education has been shown to reduce health-care utilization, improve disease control and management in our inner-city community. The purpose of our study was to evaluate the impact of outpatient group asthma education in disease control and healthcare utilization. Methods This was a six-month pre-post analysis of patients managed in the pulmonary clinic from April 2016 to April 2018. Patients undergoing group education were included. A group asthma education program was implemented in April 2016 and includes two scheduled 2 hours/each group sessions for 8-12 patients. Standardized verbal, written, hands-on training and pre and post-test by certified asthma educators (pharmacist/respiratory therapist) and pulmonologist is done. Sessions are offered in English and Spanish. Primary outcomes measured were Emergency Room (ER) visits and hospital admissions. Secondary outcomes were asthma control as per the Asthma Control Test (ACT) score and steroid use. Results 88 patients attended group education classes during the study period; 82 (83%) completed two group sessions. There were 75% females, and the average age of the group was 58 years old with 73% of Hispanic followed by African-American (25%). All patients had persistent asthma, and comorbid conditions were seen in up to 20% of patients, obstructive sleep apnea and gastroesophageal reflux disease were the most common. Average BMI in the group was 31.60+/-7.08. 41% of our patients were smoker 8% active, others former tobacco abuse. Group asthma education had a significant impact in ER utilization (p=0.002), asthma control by ACT score>=20) (p=0.0043) and systemic steroid requirement (p=.0005). Comprehension and learning of teaching material as per the pre-post test was also improved. The number of patients requiring hospitalization did not decrease (p= 0.18). Table 1 Conclusions and Clinical Implications Asthma group education decreased health-care utilization and improved asthma care/management as evidenced by decreased steroid use and improved asthma control. Additional benefit was a better disease understanding and medication use. In our inner-city community, we serve patients with multiple comorbidities and many social barriers. There are limited resources for dedicated extended individual asthma teaching. Our results suggests that targeted, comprehensive group education, ideally in the patient's preferred language is an effective, feasible and efficient way to use existing resources. We need to follow those patients to evaluate if the impact of education is sustainable or they could require further re-education.
