AB  - Purpose: Homeless people are a heterogeneous and hard to reach population who are known to suffer significant physical health problems and whose circumstances pose challenges for accessing health services. Physical activity is important for sustaining health and group exercise has been shown to be beneficial for self-esteem and confidence. "A Mile in Her Shoes" is a volunteer-led charity which provides physical activity and clothing towomenwho have lived expe rience of homelessness. The purpose of this research was primarily to explore the experiences of women using the ser vice and establish how running has impacted their lives. A secondary objective was to use the findings to feedback to the charity regarding service design. This study is relevant to physiotherapistsworking in urban areas where homeless people may make up a proportion of their clinical caseload, as it provides insight into how community based physical activity programmes can impact on the wellbeing of this vulnerable population. Method(s): All regular attenders of the running groups were invited to participate by the group leaders. In total, were interviewed. All interviews were digitally recorded, transcribed verbatim and analysed using thematic analysis. Trustworthiness was maximised by cross-referencing emergent themes across researchers and using direct quotes tosupport findings. Clarity was continually sought throughoutthe interviews. Result(s): The interviewees gave detailed insight into thebenefits of the group, indicating the servicewas well receivedby the women who accessed it. They collectively reportedincreased confidence and happiness, and an increased aware-ness of physical and mental health through social interactions. With physical heath, the women specifically highlighted animprovement in fitness and a reduction in some health relatedissues. They also made useful suggestions as to how thegroups could be developed in the future to maximise satis-faction and engagement for the vulnerable women it targets. Conclusion(s): This study concludes that volunteer-ledjogging groups for women with a lived experience of home-lessness are of value to its target population. The service usersbelieved that the groups helped both their physical and mental health. This suggests that community-based, volunteer-ledgroup physical activity interventions can have positive out-comes for some of society's most vulnerable and hard toreach people, who otherwise may be overlooked by main-stream services. Future work in this area could explore thepartnership of physiotherapy departments and the voluntarysector, which create opportunities for interventions and ser-vices to improve the health of vulnerable populations, suchas women with the lived experience of homelessness. Implications: Engagement in physical activity may be afirst step for vulnerable people taking control of their health. Thorough evaluation of unique services such as "A Mile inHer Shoes", insight can be gained into howthe voluntary sec-tor and healthcare professionals can optimise engagement ofvulnerable people in physical activity and healthcare services. This can help to prevent injury/illness, and aid in recovery andrehabilitation. Service providers should recognise that givingvulnerable women a voice in the design of services that targetthem is essential in ensuring the services are appropriate sothey are utilised.
