AB  - Research has well established that faith and spirituality play a positive role in people's mental and physical health. Ministers are the voice and guide of faith for many people, so their well-being has bearing on the spirituality and well-being of many others. Burnout among clergy is a growing problem. Studies have identified loneliness and isolation as contributing factors in burnout, but there is limited research on what elements or circumstances lead to clergy isolation and there is an absence of representation among the Christian Churches/Churches of Christ. This research sought to answer the following questions: How do ministers and their spouses within the Christian Churches/Churches of Christ experience connection within the congregations in which they serve? How does it differ from the way the congregants experience connection within the church body? How does the minister's perception compare with the congregants' perceptions of how the minister experiences connection within the church body? This grounded theory study explored these questions through open-ended interviews with individuals from two sample groups: 1) ministers and their spouses and 2) long-term, husband-wife members of the same congregations. There was also a follow-up focus group with a panel of ministers. Theoretical saturation was achieved after 12 interviews (6 couples each), or 24 participants. Along with the five ministers in the focus group, the total number of participants in the study was 29. Analysis of the interviews identified seven main of themes in the following areas: consumerism, leadership, view of minister, view of church relationships, vulnerability, crisis, and support. Several implications are discussed including the importance of strong church leadership, small group involvement, and ministers modeling openness and asking for help. The researcher suggests the following interventions: organized responsive services to ministers in crisis and preventative support services for clergy, intentional proactive educational efforts among churches, church leadership and ministers, as well as leadership training and development. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
