AB  - Theorists such as Kohut (1971) and Wurmser (1978) believe that drug addiction is an expression of narcissistic rage against the self, and that this rage, if ameliorated, would reduce the need for drugs. This study tested the following hypotheses: (1) Participation in an anger management program is significantly associated with reductions in both experienced and expressed anger in a sample of drug abusers in a therapeutic community; (2) participation in an anger management program by the sample of drug abusers in a therapeutic community is significantly associated with increases in self-esteem; (3) participation in an anger management program by the sample of drug abusers in a therapeutic community is significantly associated with decreases in depression; (4) participation in an anger management program by the sample of drug abusers in a therapeutic community is significantly associated with increases in treatment responsivity. Fifty male and 43 female drug addicted residents of a therapeutic community were randomly assigned to three groups and administered paper-and-pencil measures of anger (State-Trait Anger Expression Inventory, Research Edition), depression (State-Trait Depression Adjective Check Lists), self-esteem (Coopersmith Self-Esteem Inventory), and treatment responsivity (Recovery Attitude and Treatment Evaluation Questionnaire-II) immediately before the start of the first anger management workshop and one week after the workshop. The experimental group (n = 34) attended the workshop. The control group (n = 29) received an attention placebo, and the no treatment group (n = 30) was not treated. Separate repeated measures ANOVAs for each of the hypotheses revealed no significant difference between group treatment effects pre to posttreatment; the hypotheses were not supported. Possible reasons for this lack of support include: (1) the workshop was not intensive enough within the context of the larger program of treatment at the TC to be effective; (2) anger plays a smaller role in the etiology and maintenance of addiction than was hypothesized. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
