AB  - The effects of working with clients who have been victimized have been investigated under several theoretical models such as burnout (Freudenberger & Robbins, 1979), secondary traumatic stress (Figley, 1999), countertransference reactions (Dalenberg, 2000), and vicarious traumatization (McCann & Pearlman, 1990). However, a review of the literature found only two studies that examined therapists treating perpetrators of violent and antisocial acts, such as those who treat sex offenders. The primary aim of the study was to examine whether sex offender treatment providers report higher levels of VT than do other therapists. This study surveyed 202 therapists from four groups of treatment providers. A total of 53 participants responded to the survey including 17 from the Texas Department of Criminal Justice Sex Offender Treatment Program, 10 community-based sex offender treatment providers, 17 community-based treatment providers who identify themselves as generalists, and 9 therapists who treat trauma victims. Participants completed a consent form, the Traumatic Stress Institute Belief Scale (TSIBS), the Impact of Events Scale (IES), the Symptom Checklist-90-R (SCL-90-R), and a demographic questionnaire. It was hypothesized that (a) therapists who treat sex offenders will report more VT symptoms than those who do not, (b) sex offender therapists with greater experience will exhibit fewer VT symptoms, (c) therapists who have personal histories of trauma, sexual or non-sexual, will exhibit greater degrees of VT symptoms, and (d) therapists who seek supervision or personal therapy will exhibit lesser degrees of VT symptoms. Although there were significant group effects on three of the four measures of VT, none of the post hoc analyses yielded significant differences between any of the pairs of therapist groups on any of the measures of VT. Furthermore, despite the hypothesis that more experienced therapists would report lower levels of VT, the study failed to find any relationships between years of practice and any VT symptoms. Nor did the study demonstrate any relationship between therapists' history of prior trauma and VT symptomology. Finally, the study was unable to provide evidence that either supervision or personal psychotherapy mitigates VT. The findings of this study should be viewed with caution considering the low number of respondents and low statistical power. Future research should attempt to replicate this study with a larger number of participants. Additionally, a comparison group of generalist corrections-based therapists should be utilized to determine if VT effects are due to sex offender treatment or providing therapy within a corrections environment. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
