AB  - There were several objectives of this study: (1) to determine whether gender differences exist in college students' use of self-help groups; (2) to determine what types of self-help groups students would attend; (3) to assess whether there were differences between students' willingness to use self-help groups and perceptions of key informants; and (4) to assess whether prior help-seeking experiences had an impact on students' intentions to use self-help groups. Interviews with a random sample of 270 college students and a selected sample of 52 key informants showed that Ss were more willing to use self-help groups than key informants predicted. Ss were especially willing to use self-help groups for difficulties related to physical handicap, sexual assault, childhood abuse, AIDS, and drug abuse, but not for relationship issues. Female Ss were more likely than male Ss to use a self-help group, especially for childhood abuse, child-rearing issues, alcohol abuse, and depression. Ss were more likely to use a self-help group if they had been in a self-help group previously, but not if they had previously been in counseling. The findings suggest that educating campus professionals will increase the use of self-help groups. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
