AB  - An innovative 12-week treatment protocol that integrates visual exposure and processing techniques with traditional cognitive behavioral strategies was developed and implemented with a group of chronic substance dependent inpatients. Working from a provisional treatment manual, two registered art therapists were trained to deliver the treatment to a group of clients (N = 8) with co-occurring substance abuse and mental health disorders. Results showed that clients increased their verbalization about emotions related to their substance abuse, increased their ability to tolerate strong negative affect, showed improved interpersonal interactions, and reported a decrease in cravings. While clients did not show improvement in general emotional/physical distress related to substance use or in making more permanent cognitive changes related to triggers, they did both increase verbalization about emotions related to their substance abuse and decrease their urge to drink and take drugs. These preliminary results suggest that integrating a sensory-based experiential component to traditional cognitive behavioral treatment methods may help clients improve affect management skills and may facilitate cognitive-affective integration that reduces craving intensity. [ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR] Copyright of Best Practice in Mental Health is the property of Follmer Group and its content may not be copied or emailed to multiple sites or posted to a listserv without the copyright holder's express written permission. However, users may print, download, or email articles for individual use. This abstract may be abridged. No warranty is given about the accuracy of the copy. Users should refer to the original published version of the material for the full abstract. (Copyright applies to all Abstracts.)
