AB  - Studied the clinical predictors of abstinence or drinking outcome for 74 male alcoholics (aged 25–53 yrs) during a 3-yr prospective study. Clinical control was performed after detoxification and after 1 yr, 2 yrs, and 3 yrs. 34 Ss abused alcohol throughout the study. 17 Ss had a few short periods of heavy drinking during the 3 yrs. 23 Ss remained abstinent or broke their abstinence a few times during the analyzed period, but without any further physical or psychological effects. The occurrence of other mental disturbances was found to be a negative predictor for all Ss with alcohol dependence syndrome. Moderately intense anxiety and depression were observed after intoxication, but the symptoms decreased or disappeared in Ss who remained abstinent. Regular participation in Alcoholics Anonymous groups and in outpatient treatment was correlated positively with lasting abstinence. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
