AB  - The present study examined what mental health services are provided to persons with schizophrenia residing in an intermediate care facility for persons with mental illness (ICFMI) and what factors predict the provision of mental health services. One hundred residents living in an ICFMI with a chart diagnosis of either schizophrenia or schizoaffective disorder were randomly selected to participate. Diagnoses were confirmed by structured clinical interview (SCID). Seventy-eight individuals were given a diagnosis of either schizophrenia (n = 39) or schizoaffective disorder (n = 39) and included in the analyses. Results indicate that 53% of all residents received any speciality mental health services beyond medication (e.g., group therapy, day program). Persons with the least severe psychiatric disorders, and persons with high levels of motivation for overall care received the most facility mental health services. Hospitalization rates of residents during their stay at the facility was 35%. Residents most likely to be readmitted to the psychiatric hospital were young men with a history of homelessness and who were likely to refuse facility activities, mental health services and medication management. Results suggest that continued deinstitutionalization efforts have done little to effect mental health care for persons with the most severe psychiatric disorders. Policy implications are discussed. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
