AB  - This study centers on the teaching and use of reality therapy and behavior modification techniques to educate and enable homeless and schizophrenic clients so they can more effectively become and remain medication compliant, and ultimately, so they can leave the shelter and lead productive lives. Case studies were developed on all the participants. Initially, the group consisted of 10 persons-4 men and 6 women-diagnosed with schizophrenia (paranoid type). The youngest person was 19 years old and the oldest was 42. The study used 10 hourly group sessions and lasted 10 weeks. A demographic pre/post-questionnaire was orally administered to each client to determine the level of compliance. Behaviors were observed and recorded by this researcher and another clinician. Seven clients participated in all 10 sessions. After a 3-month follow-up period, 6 participants were medication compliant and employed. Of the 3 who did not fully participate, one was hospitalized after not taking proper medication, another left the shelter, and the third voluntarily admitted herself to a hospital because of suicidal ideation. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
