AB  - The Committee considers the steady demographic trend towards aging of populations in the developed countries, the already marked evidence of a similar trend in the developing countries, and the serious implications of these trends for all aspects of human life. It is emphasized that the biological age of the individual, as distinct from his chronological or calendar age, is very important, and studies concerned with the process of aging in relation to the genetic, immunological, and adaptive patterns in aging were given special attention in view of the possibility that the aging rate might eventually be slowed down. Such studies should throw more light on the problems of age associated diseases, and result in a more comfortable life for the elderly and the aged. The major problem areas of aging and aged people from the health point of view extend from physical and mental health in their broader aspects to a multiplicity of nutritional, environmental, social, and economic factors that have a direct impact on health. Special emphasis is given by the Committee to the problems of ill health, and especially long term illness, as expressed by their consequences, such as impairment, disability, incapacity, and dependency. Attention is also given to the particular problems of the aged in the developing countries. The basic concepts involved in meeting the multiple needs of the aged are stated, and the Committee recognizes the necessity for planning and organizing integrated systems for the delivery of services to the aged. The Committee was unable to reach a definite conclusion as to whether it is desirable that geriatrics should be recognized in all countries as a medical specialty in its own right. However, it is agreed that the question should be seriously examined in view of the considerable time that would elapse before any decision on this matter could have any effect on the delivery of services. It is therefore urged that in certain countries where geriatrics is not yet recognized, it should be tentatively established as a special branch of medicine. The Committee recognizes the value for the aged of social security and national health service programmes in delivering integrated personal health services, including preventive and curative care, rehabilitation, and permanent surveillance. These aspects are dealt with extensively by a Joint ILO/WHO Expert Committee on Personal Health Care and Social Security.
