AB  - Research on peer psychotherapy organizations has emphasized their affinity with more conventional forms of "group therapy" & has attributed their success to such broad processes as confession, catharsis, group support, & removal of stigma. This view neglects the feature of these groups that members take most seriously--their complex & often sharply contrasting ideologies. Ethnographic research suggests that cognitive changes induced by the appropriation of a specific ideology may have important therapeutic effects related in very precise ways to the needs of each group's client population. Case studies of the three most prominent peer therapy organizations (Alcoholics Anonymous, Recovery, Inc, & Synanon) are presented in support of this view. AA.
