AB  - ; This article presents information on the Group Personality Projective Test (GPPT) within a prison population. The GPPT was selected for further research for several reasons. First, it appears that psychologists in several large penal institutions view the GPPT as a potentially valuable tool for discriminating among criminal subgroups, and this test indeed has been administered to prisoners for evaluative purposes in the absence of substantiating evidence. Second, there is no other instrument that appears to discern effectively criminals who demonstrate varying patterns of aggressive behavior. Third, the format of the GPPT meets present demands for relative objectivity, speed and economy. These qualifications are essential for a psychological test to be of practical utility within most penal systems, for the ratio of staff psychologists to inmates is very low.
