AB  - BACKGROUND: Government policy identifies a need to improve the quality of life (QoL) of people living with and beyond cancer. Gynaecological cancer patients (GCPs) have a low 5 year survival rate, and ovarian cancer patients in particular report significant QoL concerns. Stress‐reduction programmes, such as yoga, have long been believed to have beneficial effects on physical and emotional health. METHODS: A randomised clinical trial to explore the effect of yoga on QoL was offered to GCPs (n = 44). Measures included the EORTC QLQ‐C30 and weekly visual analogue scales for QoL, pain, anxiety and fatigue. Focus groups were used to evaluate the intervention, and audiorecordings were analysed using Interpretative Phenomenological Analysis. RESULTS: Quantitative analysis found a significant correlation between QoL and week of intervention, suggesting that as time (number of weeks) went on quality of life improved for patients. In addition, QoL positively correlated with attendance ‐ the more classes patients attended the greater the improvement in QoL. Qualitative analysis revealed a number of salient themes surrounding the benefits of taking part in the yoga intervention. These included: 'The ability to give and receive social support outside of traditional support group settings', 'Giving patients “permission” to take part in physical activity' and 'Giving patients tools to use for the future'. CONCLUSION: It appears that yoga can improve the QoL of GCPs. Yoga is an intervention that is highly valued by the patients and warrants additional research with a larger sample size to further explore the effects found in this pilot study.
