KW  - Major Depression
KW  - Symptoms
PY  - 2021
DA  - 2021///
Y1  - 2021///
AB  - Intimate partner violence (IPV) is highly prevalent in the United States, especially amongst South Asian Communities. This is alarming as IPV has severe mental and physical health consequences, especially poor social and interpersonal functioning, depressive symptoms, emotion dysregulation and suicidal thoughts and behaviors (STBs). Additionally, South Asian women who have experienced IPV are particularly vulnerable due to the additional effects of acculturative stress, which can exacerbate these consequences. Dialectical behavior therapy (DBT) skills group has shown improvements in mood symptoms, STB and emotional dysregulation. This study aims to examine the feasibility, acceptability, and preliminary effects of an adapted, 5-week DBT skills group for South Asian women who have experienced IPV. Ten women were recruited from a community center for South Asian women who have experienced IPV. Due to the coronavirus pandemic, the community center had to close, and participants only received three out of the five groups. Seven women were included in the analyses (one dropped out and two were ineligible). The study had a low attrition rate of 13%. Participants reported a total average score of 95% on their expectations of the treatment's credibility and the level of success. Qualitative analyses showed that participants found the treatment helpful, especially the mindfulness module and the three states of mind. There was mixed feedback on whether the DBT skills group was culturally appropriate with some participants stating that the intervention required a basic level of education and understanding of mental health, which women in their home countries may not have had. A paired sample t-test found that there was no significant difference in scores across timepoints for depressive symptoms, STBs or emotion regulation. There was a decrease in subscales scores related to difficulties in engaging in goal-directed cognition and behavior and lack of access to strategies for feeling better when distressed. The group leader provided qualitative feedback that she found it helpful to have concrete skills that she could teach and that the assessments and rating scales used for research and in the homework were not appropriate for this population. She reported that she would continue to implement this intervention once the center reopens. In conclusion, participants found the treatment to be feasible, as evidenced by low attrition rates, and acceptable, shown by the high scores on a measure of treatment credibility. Although there was no difference in symptom reduction, there was in an increase in the emotion regulation subscale scores that suggested use of skills was helpful in engaging in effective, goal-directed behavior. However, due to the lack of data and early discontinuation of the group, no conclusive findings can be made on symptom reduction. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
