AB  - Evaluates a 6-month outpatient program developed to improve abstinence & other health & social problems of heroin-addicted persons hospitalized in an urban academic medical center's medical or surgical unit & referred from a substance abuse consult service. Treatment intervention consisted of methadone & daily group therapy, individual case management, medical & psychiatric consultation, & social services. The first 67 patients referred were followed for 6 months. Outcome measures included retention in treatment, urine drug screens, & health & social indicators. Patients averaged 19 weeks in treatment, with 52% of patients completing the 6-month program. Urine toxicology screens showed a reduction of illicit drug use. Patients improved deficits in health & social indicators by obtaining medical coverage, keeping outpatient medical appointments, & improving their housing conditions. Findings suggest that this combination of intensive psychosocial treatment with opioid substitution following an acute illness substantially impacts treatment outcomes. Implications for service delivery & health care economics are discussed. 2 Tables, 71 References. Adapted from the source document.
