AB  - This dissertation examined how treatment length (dosage) of the Residential Substance Abuse Treatment (RSAT) programs within the Nebraska Department of Correctional Services (NDCS) affected involvement in serious misconduct after successful program completion. Four correctional institutions within NDCS offer RSAT programming. In February of 2013, the programs underwent a change in treatment length that shortened the programs from nine months to six months. Propensity score matching, regression analyses, and survival analyses were utilized to examine the effect of dosage for the 9-month and 6-month treatment groups by gender. For both the male and female samples, findings indicated that treatment dosage was not a significant predictor of misconduct. Regarding the male sample, mental health, criminal histories, and other factors were significant predictors of serious misconduct involvement. For the female sample, age, race, and visitation after program completion were significant predictors of the commission of serious misconduct. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2018 APA, all rights reserved)
