DA  - 2007/03//
Y1  - 2007/03//
AB  - This study assessed efforts to reform the mental health services system in Israel. It aimed to understand why those efforts, which involved transferring the locus of treatment and care from the mental health hospital system to the community, have consistently failed over the last 35 years. An attempt is made to identify factors that have hindered the efforts to change the policy, and factors that might have facilitated the planned reforms. Following a description of the scope of mental health problems and trends in mental health policy and services in Israel, the major interest groups and main issues involved in the efforts to reform the mental health service system are assessed. Conflicts between the parties revolved around issues of budget, control, and professional and organizational autonomy. Analysis indicates that the proponents of the reforms failed to mobilize a strong coalition, and that the leadership of the Ministry of Health lacked a strong commitment to the reforms. Failure to implement the reforms was also partly due to the structure of services, their historical and organizational background, and the traditional orientation that views curative medicine and hospitals as the hub of the system. Suggestions are offered for strategies that might facilitate the planned change in the mental health service system. In addition, the main factors that may explain the failure of efforts to implement the reform are highlighted. These factors relate to the social and political circumstances of Israeli society during the period under investigation, the lack of salience attributed to mental health reform, and low public priority relative to other issues of societal concern, as well as to the marginality of the mentally disabled population, their traditional exclusion, and their stigma.
