AB  - Prevalence data for the United States indicate that autism rates have risen steadily since the 1960s, when estimates hovered around 5 per 10,000 persons. This change has largely been attributed to evolving and broadened diagnostic criteria, along with public awareness and greater recognition of autism spectrum disorder (ASD). ASDs can be difficult to differentiate from other developmental disorders, particularly in younger populations. Cognitive and behavioral symptoms consistent with an ASD diagnosis in childhood overlap significantly with a wide range of medical and psychiatric conditions. The DSM-5 has brought about changes to the classification of ASD features, and, with these changes, new assessment and diagnostic tools will be required for strong specificity and sensitivity. This chapter discusses ASD and its prevalence rates. Following, it explains the diagnosing and assessing ASD from a differential diagnosis and comorbidity perspective. The chapter addresses the school mental health professional's role and intervention, with attention paid to the use of response to intervention for children with autism spectrum disorders. It reviews the targeted classroom interventions of schedules, story-based interventions, modeling, and joint attention. The chapter identifies the opportunities to further support students with autism spectrum disorders in collaboration with parents, teachers, and the community at large. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
