AB  - Background: Severe alcoholic hepatitis (AH) is a distinct clinical syndrome associated with high short-term mortality. The long-term efficacy of steroids in these patients is questionable. Despite compelling new evidence that liver transplantation (LT) is an excellent therapy for AH in carefully selected patients, most liver transplant centers in the USA mandate a 6-month wait in patients with alcoholic liver disease. We investigated the practice patterns and opinions regarding the use of LT for severe AH. Method(s): A short questionnaire was developed to determine practices and opinions with respect to LT for AH. A list of adult transplant centers was obtained from the Scientific Registry of Transplant Recipients website. Following IRB approval, an individualized survey link was created and distributed using REDCap (Research Electronic Data Capture) via email to the medical director or lead transplant hepatologist. Responses were collected within REDCap. Statistical analysis was performed using SPSS. Categorical variables were compared using the Fisher's exact test. Continuous variables were compared using Levene's t-test for equal variances and student's t-test. Results were significant if p<0.05. Result(s): 109 centers were contacted and 45 transplant centers completed the survey. 23 centers (51.1%) reported transplanting patients for severe AH. Post-LT survival was excellent with 17 centers (73.9%) reporting 1-year post-transplant survival of greater than 90% (Figure 1), mirroring the rates seen in alcoholic cirrhosis. Criteria used to select patients for LT at these centers included: first presentation with AH (21 centers, 91.3%); completion of a psychosocial evaluation (20 centers, 87.0%); and a strong social support system (23 centers, 100%). Of the 22 centers that had never transplanted for AH, 11 centers (50%) evaluated patients with severe AH for transplant. Interestingly, a larger number of centers (16 centers, 72.7%) indicated that they would not consider carrying out liver transplant for AH in the future, with the most commonly cited reason being perceived high risk of relapse (9 centers, 41.0%) (Figure 2). There was no significant difference between centers that had transplanted for severe AH and those that had not with respect to age of program, transplant volume, post-transplant survival, and MELD at transplant. Post-transplant services offered to patients who underwent LT for AH was variable. Only 15 centers (65.25) offered psychiatric services, and support group therapy was only provided by 8 centers (35%). Conclusion(s): LT is increasingly being offered to selected patients presenting with severe AH in the USA with exceptional outcomes. Post-transplant psychiatric support which is important in these patients is still inadequate at most transplant institutions. Nevertheless, LT should be considered in well-selected patients with severe AH. [Table Presented] [Table Presented]Copyright © 2018 AGA Institute. All rights reserved.
