AB  - The purpose of this study was to examine the impact of a culturally sensitive, community-based group intervention, using social support and active coping to improve functional outcomes, i.e., employment and mental health, for African Americans and Latinos with disabilities. Forty-six African American and 34 Latino clients of the Rehabilitation Services Administration in Washington, D.C., were recruited. They were assigned either to an intervention group--social support or psychosocial competence--or a comparison group. Seven psychoeducational group sessions trained participants in how to use either social support or active coping specifically for job attainment. Two data collection sessions were held for comparison and intervention-group participants. Pre- and post-tests were administered the first and last sessions. Measures included The Personal Resources Questionnaire (PRQ), Behavioral Attributes of Active Coping Scale (BAPC-C), Beck Depression Inventory (BDI), and the Walker/Asbury Structured Interview System, which assessed employability. Analysis of covariance were computed to compare groups on outcomes. Work efficacy improved significantly for either intervention but not for the comparison group. Satisfaction with social support was marginally enhanced for intervention-group participants but not for those in the comparison group. Depression decreased for the comparison group and did not decrease for those participating in the intervention. There were marginally significant interactions between intervention status (comparison or intervention) and ethnicity. Latinos improved more than African Americans on social support and work attitudes. In examining the functional outcomes by ethnicity, Latinos scored higher on the active coping and the probability of holding a job measures than African Americans. In summary, the social support and psychosocial intervention enhanced satisfaction with social support and beliefs in employability for African Americans and Latinos with disabilities. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
