AB  - Examples of community researchers involved in the projects described in the book include young people, older people, indigenous and Roma people, residents of neighbourhoods and mental health service users. Other issues raised include: difficulties in maintaining boundaries between research and intervention (Chapter 8 on HIV research in South Africa); challenges of engaging community researchers in data analysis (Chapter 7 on research on housing estates in Bradford, UK); and issues with older people as researchers assuming a positivist methodology and not seeing age discrimination (Chapter 3 on authenticity and validity in research about age discrimination and urban social interactions in the UK). [...]it does raise the question as to whether what is often called 'citizen science' (that involves 'community members' co-designing research projects and/or collecting data via quantitative surveys or scientific experiments) and arts-based projects (for example, researching an issue via co-designed exhibitions or community philosophy) count as 'community research' and if not, why not?
