AB  - Background: Numerous legal articles have been written about the effectiveness of drug courts relative to incarceration in reducing drug-related recidivism, arguably due to treatment provided in drug courts. However, few studies have examined precisely what substance abuse "treatment" means in a drug court context. Additionally, there is reason to think that many drug court participants are not being provided evidence-based treatment, especially for opiate addiction. A national study by Matusow et al. (2013) found that less than half of drug courts permit agonist medication-assisted treatment (MAT), and only 56% of drug courts permit any kind of MAT. Method(s): This study consist of interviews with 17 Indiana drug court and veteran court judges, representing a total of 20 drug and verterans courts. Each interview lasted approximately one hour. Topics included treatment team composition; treatment decision-making process; typical treatment protocols; treatment methods for pregnant women; court treatment policies; perceptions of the safety and efficacy of counseling, support groups, and MAT; commonly abused drugs; court funding; fees; treatment policies for co-occurring conditions; and public perception of drug and veterans courts. Result(s): Drug court and veterans court treatment teams typically consist of the judge, law enforcement, probation officers, one or two counselors, and a case manager. In only two cases was a physician included on the treatment team. Other results include widespread lack of accurate information about the efficacy of methadone and buprenorphine treatments; strong bias against MAT for opiate addiction; and widespread lack of knowledge about existence of Vivitrol treatment. Discouragement of MAT or bans on MAT are common for pregnant women. Widespread opposition exists to the new SAMHSA requirement to permit MAT as a condition of Federal funding for drug courts. However, veterans courts were more likely to permit MAT than non-veterans courts. Despite negative perceptions of MAT, medication for other mental health conditions is either required or recommended. Typical addiction treatment consist of 2-3 times weekly intensive outpatient counseling and a requirement to attend twelve-step groups or non-spiritual alternatives. All drug and veterans court participants must pay participation fees (often without low-income exceptions) and urine analysis test costs. No drug courts cover the cost of treatment; participants must pay out of pocket or through their own insurance (commercial or Medicaid). Short-term imprisonment is used as a sanction; but multiple courts require longer-term incarceration for pregnant women who relapse. Participant poverty, lack of transportation assistance, and homelessness were described as significant treatment barriers. Most judges believe a severe under-supply of local half-way houses and in-patient rehabilitation centers exists in Indiana. Widespread misinformation about MAT found in this study suggests that judges need more education about comparative treatment method effectiveness, especially for opioid dependence. According to study results, the primary information source for drug court judges about treatment methods is the annual National Association of Drug Court Professionals conference. More judicial education is needed. Federal and state governments should provide financial incentives for drug courts and veterans courts to include physicians on their treatment teams, as well as funding for participant transportation, housing, and treatment. The study is ongoing.
