AB  - Introduction: Evidences of efficacy of Self-Management (SM) programs on stroke survivors quality of life and self-efficacy exist in literature, but there is variability in program type, timing and setting. Purpose(s): To develop and evaluate the impact of a standardized TPE program on empowerment of SM for stroke survivors in the early post-acute phase. Method(s): Design: controlled clinical trial in 3 Italian rehabilitation centers. Patient(s): stroke survivors admitted to rehabilitation ward. Inclusion criteria: first stroke, >18 ys, caregiver, moderate-severe disability (MBI-Modified Barthel Index<70), no severe communication disability and cognitive impairment (MMSE>15). Primary Outcome: patient perceived self efficacy (SSEQ-Stroke Self Efficacy Questionnaire)Assessments: T0 (enrollment), T1 (inpatient rehabilitation discharge), T2 (50-60 days after discharge): SSEQ, MBI, Short Physical Performance Battery, Geriatric Depression Scale, SF-12, patient and caregiver satisfaction, Caregiver Strain Index. At T2 also: % of homecoming, lenght of stay, territorial services use. Result(s): TPE intervention was set up defining contents, timing and modality (6 group and 3 individual sessions directed by physiatrists, physiotherapists, nurses). The program, which is focused on the training on goal setting and problem solving, is an adaptation of the Chronic Disease SM Program (Stanford). Usual care phase confirmed homogeneity of 3 centers. Intervention phase enrolled 82 patients in the intervention group, 103 in the control group. Intervention group showed improvements in SSEQ and SF-12 in younger (<75 years) and in better cognitive performances (MMSE>24) stroke survivors. Conclusion(s): LAY project analysed barriers and facilitators of SM program delivery in early postacute phase and explored the feasibility of SM support for patients, caregivers and health professionals. We will present complete data. (Figure Presented) .
