AB  - The purpose of this study was to examine the relationship between Piaget's levels of cognition and Beck's levels of dressing performance in subjects with Alzheimer's disease and related disorders (ADRD). In addition, the study tested the efficacy of using the Cognitive Developmental Approach to dementia as a theoretical framework for viewing the relationship between cognition in individuals with ADRD and their ability to perform activities of daily living, specifically dressing activities. The investigative study design was cross sectional correlational. Instruments included the Folstein Mini Mental State Exam, the Linton Piaget Test, the Beck Dressing Performance Scale, and the Observable Displays of Affect Scale. A convenience sample of ninety subjects between ages 66 and 100 was used. Inclusion criteria were: a diagnosis of ADRD, Mini Mental Status Exam score of 24 or below, physical capability of dressing, English speaking, and the ability to complete the object permanence task at the Sensorimotor Level. Research sites included Southeastern Arizona rural and urban long-term care facilities, adult care homes, and the community.Thirty subjects were tested in three Piaget Levels including Sensorimotor, Preoperational, and Concrete. Results indicate that individuals at the Sensorimotor Level require a great deal of assistance while those at the Concrete Level require little assistance. Individuals at the Preoperational Level vary in their requirements for assistance. Analysis by Spearman Correlation Coefficient between Piaget Level and dressing performance is -.73 with p =.0005. In addition, reliability analysis of the Piaget test demonstrated its significant internal consistency (alpha =.9433 overall).Affect as measured by the Observable Displays of Affect Scale, psychotropic medications, and other medical diagnoses do not seem to be correlated with Piaget Level or dressing sing performance.Study results determined the dressing performance range for each Piaget Level. Implications for nursing as a result of this study: (1) Subjects performing at the low end of their expected range could be a focus for nursing interventions that assist them to achieve a higher level of performance, and (2) Subjects at the Preoperational Level may be a particularly significant group to target for intervention because these individuals are frequently not functioning at their highest potential and may be vulnerable to excess disability.Future research should focus on individuals with ADRD at the Preoperational Level. Further intervention studies as well as instrumentation studies hold much potential to improve care given to ADRD victims.
