AB  - Data for 736 patients admitted to inpatient psychiatric services between 1983–1991, with a diagnosis of endogenous or neurotic depression, were examined to test the hypothesis that psychosocial and clinical variables influence the length of stay (LOS) in hospital treatment. Variables included age group (18–45 and 46–65 yrs old), gender, education, marital status, employment, loneliness, and severity and type of depression. Analysis revealed that being 46–65 yrs old, female, widowed, divorced, single, severely depressed, and having the diagnosis of endogenous depression were all associated with longer LOS. Age accounted for nearly 50% of LOS variations. Three significant interactions were found in relation to LOS: perceived loneliness with marital status (decreasing in level from widowed, single, divorced, and married), loneliness and gender (higher in males), and severity of depression with gender (higher in females). (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
