AB  - In (i) Hartman v South Essex Mental Health and Community Care NHS Trust; (ii) Best v Staffordshire University; (iii) Wheeldon v HSBC Bank Ltd; (iv) Green v Grimsby Scunthorpe Newspapers Ltd; (v) Moore v Welwyn Components Ltd; (vi) Melville v The Home Office, the Court of Appeal heard appeals concerning psychiatric injury caused by stress at work. Scott Baker LJ delivered the judgment. He noted that despite the decisions of the Court of Appeal in Hatton v Sutherland (2002) and the House of Lords in Barber v Somerset County Council (2004) judges were finding difficulty in applying the appropriate principles in claims arising from stress at work. Some cases were fought over many days at a cost of time and money disproportionate to the value of the claim. His Lordship set out the sixteen propositions of Hale LJ in Hatton and considered their treatment in subsequent cases. In Barber Lord Scott had said that Hale LJ successfully and accurately expressed the propositions that ought to be applied. While no other Law Lord expressly affirmed them none appeared to dispute them. Reprinted by permission of Oxford University Press
