AB  - Illicit drug addiction is extremely costly to Canadian society in terms of criminal, health, economic, and social consequences and has inspired a variety of societal responses. Treatment interventions are one approach to responding to illicit drug addiction that has been accepted as important and necessary for the management substance abuse. However, despite agreement on the utility of treatment interventions, there are significant gaps in the treatment services available across Canada. Because of this many substance abusers have difficulty accessing appropriate treatment interventions. Overcoming barriers to treatment access is a fundamental step in the development of a more effective treatment service system. This paper uses data collected in British Columbias Centre for Addiction Research Addiction Survey (2004) to explore and analyze British Columbias illicit drug users experiences with treatment access and is a multifaceted analysis that explores factors that prevent illicit drug users from receiving treatment despite efforts to get professional help as well as factors that lead other drug users not to seek treatment. Individual attitudes and characteristics that may hinder or facilitate access to treatment are also explored and will provide insight into the access needs of particular groups of drug users, such as women, youth, poly-drug users, and so on. This will contribute to the literature on substance abuse treatment that acknowledges that drug users are not a homogeneous group but have diverse treatment needs and will provide insight into the types of treatment services that need to be expanded or developed in British Columbia. [ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR] Copyright of Conference Papers -- American Society of Criminology is the property of American Society of Criminology and its content may not be copied or emailed to multiple sites or posted to a listserv without the copyright holder's express written permission. However, users may print, download, or em