AB  - Hypersexuality is a behavior pattern known to cause significant subjective distress and objective disruption, but it has been underreported in the literature. No definitive diagnostic category is available through DSM IV. Disputes abound as to whether hypersexuality parallels chemical addiction and whether the label 'disorder' is warranted. An updated assessment of the status of counselor perceptions of hypersexual behavior can facilitate improved training, clinical work, and research. The study investigated, via a written mailed questionnaire, counselors' reported perceptions of the scope, definition, diagnosis, and treatment of hypersexuality. Responses were elicited from 150 Ohio Licensed Professional Clinical Counselors, licensed since 1990, as to preferences for terminology describing hypersexual behavior, perceptions of likely antecedents, perceived incidence, course work recommended for counselor education, and preferred treatments or other interventions. Results indicated that the preferred definition includes sexual activity not associated with organic causes, brain injury, or schizophrenia, involving a few types of paraphilia and excluding others, specific to activity that involves either self stimulation or contact with other adults. Hypersexuality is seen as an out of control pattern of sexual thinking and acting, driven by anxiety reduction rather than desire, as a disorder similar to chemical addiction and closely related to obsessive-compulsiveness, and as attributable to early childhood abuse and/or neglect. Most respondents report only five or fewer clients with hypersexuality. Preferred interventions include defining behavioral boundaries, cognitive restructuring, education in recognizing and avoiding high risk situations, individual and group therapy. Chi square reveals no significant association between responses of those counselors who represent themselves as chemical addictions specialists and those who do not. Some associations are seen between responses and counselors, age, educational degree, and years of experience. Significant associations among survey item in some groups of questions mitigates against the independence of those items. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
