AB  - Describes a replication of the R. E. Drake et al (1994) study of the efficacy of an induction group in helping severely mentally ill clients make informed choices about participation in mental health research projects. The current study expanded the Drake et al study by focusing on dually diagnosed, recently homeless, predominantly African American clients (aged 18–65 yrs) from an inner-city setting. The underlying purpose of this study was to help clients fully understand the clinical and research procedures involved in a study so that they can make a truly informed decision about whether or not to participate. Results replicated those from Drake et al, showing that the research induction group resulted in informed decision-making, high rates of participation in the clinical services and in the research, and high rates of satisfaction with both vocational services and research procedures. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
