PY  - 2004
DA  - 2004/03//
Y1  - 2004/03//
AB  - When firms are acquired target shareholders seem to leave with the jam. But who pays, really pays, the bid-premia? One view is that workers pay by subsequent wage cuts and jobs lost. We investigate this claim with a sample of 62 acquisitions/restructuring events in the UK for the years 1987-95. We consider a sample of relatively large deals where the workers fate post-acquisition can be traced via newspaper reports and publicly available databases. We consider the effect of the acquisition on shareholder returns, wages paid and employment levels in the 3-5 years following the acquisition/restructuring event. More importantly we consider the trade-off between returns paid to target shareholders in the form of a bid-premia and wages cut or jobs lost in the post-acquisition firm. Our results suggest rather than workers paying the bid-premia, share-holders in the post acquisition/restructuring entity share in a form of 'joint misery' involving losses to both. Large average share-price declines relative to a size benchmark are matched by relatively modest job losses and wage declines. Indeed, workers who retain their jobs experience wage increases relative to the past, or workers in other firms in the same industry. High bid-premia are associated with modest job losses, or increases in employment, in our sample. However in the case of a sub-sample of 'hostile' acquisitions the evidence of job loss following acquisition is more mixed. Notions that takeovers act as 'breaches of trust' by employers, who renege on implicit contracts to retain older less productive workers, are contradicted by our evidence. Reprinted by permission of Blackwell Publishers
