AB  - This naturalistic study collected long-term follow-up data from 38 male veterans who had received Time-Limited Dynamic Psychotherapy (TLDP) at the Palo Alto or San Francisco Veterans Affairs Medical Centers between 1986 and 1992. Follow-up data were collected between 6 months and 6 years after TLDP termination (M = 2 years, 11 months), and included measures of current functioning as well as patient memories and assessments of their therapy and therapist. The mean age of the sample at the start of therapy was 53 years (range 30 to 77). All subjects had received a DSM-III-R Axis I diagnosis, including 50% with a substance abuse diagnosis, and 71% had an Axis II diagnosis. Fifty-six per cent had a service-connected disability. At follow-up, the mean level of psychological functioning was slightly better than at termination of treatment on all but one outcome measure. Subjects with longer times since the termination of treatment were generally functioning better than subjects who had more recently completed therapy. Posttreatment gains by subjects with Axis II diagnoses were equivalent to gains by subjects without them, and subjects with cluster A and B personality disorders made significantly better posttreatment gains than subjects with cluster C disorders. Sixty-two per cent of the sample pursued additional psychotherapy after termination. Subjects who had received high change ratings from their TLDP therapists at termination showed significantly lower rates of post-TLDP treatment utilization than subjects who had received low therapist change ratings (35% vs. 89%, respectively). The 'no additional therapy' group was functioning better than the 'additional therapy' group at follow-up. In a retrospective analysis of the therapies, patient ratings of therapy effectiveness were significantly positively correlated with a focus on TLDP-congruent topics and with the level of positive feelings between patient and therapist, and were significantly negatively correlated with (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
