AB  - The target of his attack is the widespread belief that what fueled the Depression - era growth of Social Credit in Alberta was the allegiance of independent farmers and small commercial proprietors: the "petite bourgeoisie" of Marxist thought. (This view has its contemporary form in the conviction of journalists such as Allan Fotheringham that Social Credit -- or at least the senescent embodiment that migrated to the Pacific coast to retire -- is the quintessential political party of, by, and for... used - car dealers.) To be sure, [Edward Bell] in this book revisits much of the published literature in political science on the advent of Social Credit in western Canada. In this process he pays particular attention (see esp. 21 - 34) to a classic interpretation of the movement, C. B. MacPherson's Democracy in Alberta (1953). Yet his aim is not to revise or amend the received message. Instead, Bell's careful insistence on evidence for each assertion about the appeals of Social Credit effectively undermines the aggregated weight of previous analyses. From this record, Bell nevertheless erects a likeness of Premier William Aberhart, by the end of his first mandate, as a junior - grade social democrat. Not only was Aberhart not a conservative defender of capitalism at its moment of greatest vulnerability, in Bell's narrative the Alberta leader of Social Credit proposes reforms that amount to "a clear departure from the existing way of organizing the economy and producing and distributing wealth" (128). No wonder he won the hearts and minds of his province's struggling laborers and cultivators.
