AB  - Community mental health professionals (CMHP) use various types of integrated treatment and therapeutic services for women with bipolar and substance abuse disorder (BSAD). Professionals tend to utilize various treatment methods such as: psychotherapy, pharmacotherapy, group therapy, integrated treatment to treat the co-occurring disorders. In some cases, professionals would treat the disorders separately by prescribing medication and recommending therapy while others professionals would only utilize medications to help women recover. The purpose of this research study was to explore the culture differences in CMHP integrated treatment and therapeutic services utilized for women with BSAD and to provide insights on the existent relapse rate of out-patient and in-patient treatment. The researcher utilized generic qualitative methodology with the thematic analysis approach to explore the CMHP experiences. The population for this study was CMHP who provide integrated treatment and therapeutic services to women with BSAD in Mississippi. The participants came from a diverse array of ethnicities, ages, and professionals who have 5 to 10 years of education and have provided treatment to women with BSAD from 3 to 25 years. A diverse array of participants was utilized to contribute various responses to the study's findings. The sample size recruited was 9 CMHP. After interviewing the CMHP, this researcher found the participants utilize various methods of treatment for women. In addition, the findings concluded the treatment approaches utilized by the CMHP for women with BSAD helped women become more knowledgeable of their diagnosis, aware of the signs and symptoms that trigger relapse, develop coping skills, regain social skills and self- esteem, and learn ways to remain compliant on their medications to decrease the relapse rate in the community. Copies of dissertations may be obtained by addressing your request to ProQuest, 789 E. Eisenhower Parkway, P.O. Box 1346, Ann Arbor, MI 48106-1346. Telephone 1-800-521-3042; e-mail: disspub@umi.com
