AB  - Purpose: Alcohol and other drug use disorders (i.e., substance use disorders, or SUD) have been increasingly recognized as chronic medical conditions. Patients typically require multiple episodes of treatment over several years to achieve full sustained remission. Despite recent scientific developments, relatively little is known about professional continuing care service (PCCS) utilization among emerging adults (i.e., 18-25 year olds)-a qualitatively and clinically distinct group of patients who demonstrate particularly high rates of treatment admission. Similar to other age cohorts, data suggest emerging adults are likely to benefit from participation in mutual-help organizations (MHO), such as Alcoholics Anonymous and Narcotics Anonymous, though it is unclear whether MHOs confer unique recovery benefit over and above PCCS. In order to inform and enhance broad continuing care recommendations for emerging adults, we examined rates of PCCS utilization, as well as the unique influences of PCCS and MHO attendance on SUD outcome. Method(s): In this secondary analysis of 245 emerging adults participating in a prospective naturalistic study of residential SUD treatment effectiveness, PCCS frequencies were tabulated at 3-month, 6-month, and 12-month follow-ups. Lagged hierarchical linear regression models tested for unique associations between each PCCS as well asMHO attendance at 3 months and percent days abstinent (PDA) at 6 months, and each PCCS as well as MHO attendance at 6 months and PDA at 12 months, controlling for confounds (e.g., baseline commitment to abstinence). Result(s): The most frequently reported PCCS was psychiatric medication at 3 and 6 months, and psychotherapy at 12 months. Linear regression models showed inpatient SUD treatment and baseline abstinence commitment were the only significant predictors of 6-month PDA, and only MHO attendance was a significant unique predictor of 12-month PDA. Additional subsidiary analyses examining heavy drinking days yielded non-significant results. Conclusion(s): Consistent with chronic recovery management models in adults, results here suggest a variable recovery course of PCCS utilization and related benefits for emerging adults. Baseline commitment to abstinence and further inpatient treatment appear paramount in predicting better subsequent PDA early post-treatment, while ongoing MHO attendancemay confer important community-based relapse prevention benefits later in the year following treatment.
