AB  - Purpose: Individuals with alcohol and other drug use disorders (AOD) have elevated relapse risk during the year after receiving treatment. Professional continuing care services may extend AOD treatment benefit for adults and adolescents, though less is known about continuing care among emerging adults (18-24 years old), a group of treatment seekers with unique profiles that may contribute to their poorer treatment outcomes (e.g., lower motivation for change and persisting dominance of reward-related neurocircuitry compared to older adults, and riskier social networks than adolescents). Also, despite benefit shown from emerging adults' participation in 12-step mutual-help organizations (MHOs), it is unclear whether such participation offers benefit independent of professional continuing care. Greater knowledge regarding emerging adults' professional and 12-step MHO continuing care participation may inform clinical referral and linkage efforts. Method(s): In this naturalistic treatment outcome study, emerging adults with lifetime alcohol use disorder (N = 214; 72% male; 95% White) were assessed at 1 month, 3 months, 6 months, and 12 months after residential treatment on outpatient sessions per week, percent days in residential treatment and residing in a sober living environment, SUD medication use, active 12-step MHO involvement (e.g., sum of eight activities), and continuous abstinence (dichotomized yes/no). In generalized estimating equation (GEE) Model 1, we tested the unique effect of each professional service on abstinence, and, in GEE Model 2, the unique effect of 12-step MHO involvement on abstinence over and above professional services. In both models, we controlled for demographic and baseline clinical variables that were associated with missing a follow-up and/or abstinence. Result(s): In Models 1 and 2, percent days in sober living (Odds Ratio, OR = 6.8 and 4.8, respectively) and, not surprisingly, percent days in residential treatment (OR = 10.7 and 7.2, respectively) - but not outpatient sessions per week or SUD medication - were significantly associated with abstinence. In Model 2, number of 12-step activities (OR = 1.2) was a significant, unique predictor of abstinence. Conclusion(s): Both active involvement in 12-step MHOs and recovery-supportive, professional services that link patients with these community-based resources may enhance outcomes for emerging adults with alcohol use disorder during the year after residential treatment.
