AB  - Introduction: On Easter Sunday 2010, Imperial County, California a medically underserved rural area, experienced an earthquake of magnitude 7.2 on the Richter scale. The damaged structures included an assisted living facility housing 98 older seniors, which was subsequently condemned, causing an overnight displacement. This is a historic building that originally served as a hotel (De Anza) and provided senior housing since 1999. Method(s): This is a review of actions led by ALEGRIA Adult Day Healthcare Center (ADHC), which has served the community since 2001. Key elements of the plan were securing relief efforts (i.e. food, shelter, hygiene, medications), preventing exacerbation of mental illness, and preventing new onset of mental illness. Result(s): The day of the earthquake the ADHC's administrator and property landlord inspected the building, finding that premises were able to open the next day. The daily average census prior to the earthquake was 82, during the first week after the earthquake the census decreased 50-70%. The first intervention was for social workers to locate participants that did not show up to program, as that was done the center became aware of the displaced seniors from De Anza. Services were then extended from finding them clothes to wear (i.e. staff donations), to certified nursing assistants bathing and/or shaving them at the center, nurses doing blood pressure and glucose checkups as well as contacting doctors' offices to get new medication prescriptions and rescheduling doctors' appointments, and psychotherapists doing counseling interventions as needed. Simultaneously the administrator and assistants secured provision of meals and transportation with established vendors, and participated in meetings with city officials resulting in the drafting and approval of a memorandum of understanding to provide services to uninsured displaced seniors. A month after the disaster the proportion of ADHC participants from De Anza went from 10 to 25%. Mental illness exacerbation on all previously diagnosed was contained (i.e. reported to be more anxious but no hospitalizations or medication changes were required) and there was no new onset mental illness. Conclusion(s): Disaster management after an earthquake is especially challenging since there is little warning, inability to predict potential scale of impairment of mental health services and loss of broad infrastructure. An emergency plan was led by an ADHC and implemented in collaboration with several of city decision makers. Our conclusion is that an ADHC can be an effective partner in disaster management plans at the community level. While we worked with a small sample with adequate support and monitoring it can be possible to minimize impact of disasters. More locally our experience highlights that need to develop multilevel disaster plans, and hope that our experience can serve as a template for these plans.
