AB  - Across the United States, social workers strive to provide services to military veterans who experience mental health and substance abuse disorders and, consequentially, have difficulties holding jobs, finding housing, sustaining relationships, and maintaining financial security. Data from the National Center for Veterans Analysis and Statistics indicated that in 2014, 18.2 million veterans resided in the United States, and only 9 million were enrolled in the Veterans Administration health-care system. Only 6 million of those enrolled received services. The focus of this study was to identify the perceived mental health and social work service needs of veterans within their community. A midsized suburban MidAtlantic community served as the focus for this study. Qualitative data was collected through individual interviews with nine veterans. A focus group was composed of 11 participants, seven stakeholders, in addition to four veterans who also were interviewees. Additionally, a field journal was utilized by the researcher. The results from this action research identified that veterans do not feel that community social workers and other community mental health providers are adequately trained to provide mental health services for the veteran population. Themes identified from the data, pertinent to veterans accessing community services, included the location of services provided, a veteran's period of service and service connection, and the treatment needs of veterans. The study identified gaps in service for the community's veterans, and these findings were shared with the community in a presentation. Service areas needing improvement, and ways in which the community can help fill these gaps, emerged from the interviews, focus group, and field journal. It will be imperative, moving forward, for veterans to be more involved with making positive changes in their community and engaging in the many services highlighted throughout this research. The community will need to explore how its nonVA social workers and other community mental health providers can effectively engage in training based on the military culture and the treatment needs of veteran residents. A resource guide was created that included the names of therapists trained in eye movement desensitization and reprocessing (EMDR), prolonged exposure (PE), and cognitive processing therapy (CPT). Also included was contact information for housing, job coaching, food banks, and community services for which veterans are eligible. This resource guide was given to a community nonprofit and will be available to community members by accessing the 24-hour call system. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
