AB  - The purpose of this study was to examine The Diagnostic Drawing Series (DDS), a standardized three drawing task art therapy instrument, to detect if a pattern of variables between certain patient/client groups existed. Statistically significant differences in such a pattern could indicate that the drawing procedure has potential for use in diagnosis and treatment. The DDS was used to collect artwork from 27 outpatients (13 females and 14 males) diagnosed with a substance-related disorder (SRD). The structural qualities of the art were rated according to Cohen's DDS Revised Rating Guide and Drawing Analysis Form. Four research questions were quantitatively and qualitatively examined. Results were analyzed statistically and compared using the SPSS chi-square tests. The DDSs of SRDs were compared to a control group and other diagnostic categories (multiple personality, major depression, schizophrenia, and borderline personality disorders). Findings indicated that the DDSs of control subjects and SRD outpatients were more similar than other diagnostic groups. Also, landscapes with water scenes and question marks were found in 50% of Pictures 1 and 3. Other results indicated that blending and line/quality pressure were significantly different in the DDS drawings between females and males. Additional findings included color type, groundline, line/quality pressure, and space usage were significantly different in alcohol (15) and polysubstance (12) abusers. Recommendations for possible further areas of research on this topic utilizing the DDS are presented. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
