AB  - PURPOSE: Through partnership with the GW Cancer Institute, patients served by the GW Medical Faculty Associates and the GW Hospital benefit from patient navigation across the cancer continuum. Working in collaboration with the Citywide Patient Navigation Network (CPNN), GW navigators have served over 750 patients in the last year. METHOD(S): GW navigators work together to resolve patient barriers to care. Two navigators in radiology assist all patients who have an abnormal finding in the Breast Imaging and Intervention Center. If a cancer diagnosis is confirmed, the breast screening navigators work with navigators in breast surgery, hematology/oncology, or radiation/oncology to ensure access to timely biopsy, surgery, chemotherapy and radiation treatments. A dedicated navigator in the Breast Care Center meets with every patient with a confirmed breast cancer diagnosis and guides them through treatment. A navigator shared by urology and radiation oncology serves primarily prostate and breast cancer patients, connecting patients to appropriate services and supportive care. A licensed clinical social worker based in hematology/oncology provides mentoring to navigators, counseling to patients, and coordinates distress screening and referral to a specialized Survivorship Psychiatric Services program. Two survivorship navigators assist adult cancer survivors - one navigator serves pediatric-onset survivors and the other serves adult-onset survivors through two survivorship clinics based in Internal Medicine. Navigators track their interactions with patients through manually entering data into a spreadsheet which captures aggregate patient demographics while focusing on the number and type of barriers to care, such as transportation to appointments, anxiety about stigma, and communication with medical personnel. Our navigators work in a number of different areas as we employ nurse, social work and lay navigators. Their primary activities consist of breaking down the logistical access to care issues. This includes identifying and connecting patients with transportation resources, helping patients make appointments at other institutions, follow-up with medical records departments and helping patients to apply for national or state funding for treatment. Navigators receive referrals from physicians, nurses and patient self report. We are currently rolling out distress screening on a majority of our clinics to be sure that we reach the widest breadth of patients. RESULT(S): In the last year, more than 750 individuals have been served by the GW team of navigators. A challenging case will be presented to highlight the impact of navigation at GW. For example, a brain tumor patient undergoing radiation missed several appointments, and the radiation oncologist was concerned that the patient was driving against medical advice. The navigator helped facilitate the conversation with the patient about the importance of not driving and communicated with family members. The navigator was able to secure rides for the patient using the ACS Road to Recovery program and family members. As a result of decreasing barriers, the patient completed the course of radiation treatment. It was initially difficult to get by-in from doctors, nurses, social workers, and technicians, but as navigators resolved problems and improved communication and access to care providers have become increasingly more open to using navigation services. The navigators at GW have created an environment of team work that crosses divisional barriers in the medical system. This network allows a more seamless handoff of patients ensuring quality of care and continued reductions of barriers to accessing timely care. CONCLUSION(S): Navigation plays a critical role in the provision of patient-centered care at GW. As a result of demonstrated navigation impact, salaries of 2.5 navigators along with that of the cancer center social worker are supported with operational funds. In 2013, goals of the GW navigation program are to map the patient flow and navigation process to comply with the new Commission on Cancer Navigation standard; standardize distress screening through coordination among navigators, social workers, and the Survivorship Psychiatric Services program; and pilot a database for improved case management and outcomes reporting to demonstrate the value of navigation for the cancer program. CLINICAL/RESEARCH IMPLICATIONS: The experience of navigators indicates that some patients would have never received their diagnosis or treatment if it were not for the navigation network in place at GW. The persistence and knowledge of the navigators has enabled patients to get follow-up and follow through from their medical team even when they are at a vulnerable point in care.
