AB  - Background/Objectives: This study is concerned with the consequences of psychotic disorders relating to occupational performance, following discharge from hospital, at an early stage of a person's recovery. Despite the well documented evidence that the consequences of having a diagnosed psychotic disorder greatly affect activities of daily living, there is a paucity of evidence regarding effective interventions to address this impact on daily living. This study aims to develop and evaluate an intervention to improve the ability to carry out activities of daily living for people with a diagnosed psychotic disorder, and therefore support recovery following discharge from hospital. The objectives of this study are, in relation to people with a diagnosed psychotic disorder who are recently discharged from an acute inpatient mental health service: (1) to develop an intervention to improve the ability to carry out activities of daily living; (2) to pilot and evaluate the use of the developed intervention to improve the ability to carry out activities of daily living. Method(s): The study consists of two phases. The first phase of the study involves gathering information by completing the following sub-studies: (i) A systematic review of evidence regarding use of interventions aimed at improving occupational performance for adults with a diagnosed psychotic disorder living in the community. (ii) A literature review of theory relating to occupational performance and problems of occupational performance for people with a diagnosed psychotic disorder. (iii) Focus groups with clinicians and service users regarding their experience of problems with activities of daily living following discharge from hospital. The results of the sub-studies will be synthesised to develop an evidence based intervention manual. The second phase of the study consists of piloting the intervention. A quasi experimental design consisting of an interrupted time study will be used to evaluate the intervention. Data will be collected at multiple points over time, before, during and after the intervention, using measures of occupational performance to detect whether the intervention has had an effect. Result(s): The results of phase one of the study will be presented. Discussion/Conclusions: It is hoped that this study will add to the body of knowledge regarding interventions to prevent long term disability and support recovery following a psychiatric hospital admission.
