AB  - Objective: Parent carers of children with special educational needs or disability are at higher risk of stress, depression, poorer physical health, and lower quality of life than parents of typically developing children. Researchers and parent carers co-created Healthy Parent Carers, a peer-led group-based programme to improve health and wellbeing. This trial aimed to assess whether the programme can be delivered in the community by newly trained facilitators and whether a randomised controlled trial design is feasible and acceptable to participants. Design(s): Feasibility study evaluating a parallel group randomised controlled trial design. Method(s): Parent carers were recruited and trained to facilitate the programme. Parent carer participants from six sites in South West England were allocated to either the group programme (n = 47) or to online self-directed resources only (n = 45) via a computer-generated randomisation sequence. Self-reported wellbeing and secondary outcomes were assessed at three time points. Attendance, fidelity, and participant feedback data were also collected. A measure to assess participant use of health and social care resources was developed and tested. Result(s): One hundred and ninety-three parent carers enquired about taking part. Sufficient numbers (n = 92) were recruited to run the group programme in all sites. Attendance varied with 34% parents unable to attend any sessions, and 57% attended at least two-thirds of sessions. Reasons for non-attendance were largely practical. Participants in the control arm were more likely to use online resources than those in the group intervention. Fidelity of delivery was high (90%). One participant withdrew post-randomisation, and 83 (90%) of participants completed outcome measures at six-month follow-up. The trial design was largely acceptable to participants, although some would have preferred to choose programme delivery mode and some technical problems with the online measures were reported. Unit costs of resources required for programme delivery were identified. Conclusion(s): The programme can be delivered in the community by trained facilitators. The number of enquiries indicates a high level of need for this type of intervention. Loss to follow-up was low, but attendance at sessions was variable. Programme refinement and further evaluation to reduce barriers to attendance, including examining feasibility of online delivery, is ongoing.
