AB  - Examined the hospital charts of 438 bipolar manics admitted to an acute care psychiatric inpatient ward over 12 yrs in order to identify predictors of good outcome. 80% had good and 20% poor immediate outcome. Good outcome Ss were characterized by short episode duration, older age of onset, longer hospitalization, fewer suicide thoughts, and less psychiatric and medical co-morbidity than the poor outcome group. Ss were divided into 4 treatment groups: electroconvulsive shock therapy (ECT), adequate lithium, inadequate lithium, and neither treatment. Ss experiencing good outcome were more likely to be in the adequate lithium group. The absence of co-morbidity, duration of lithium treatment more than 2 wks, and duration of episode of less than 1 mo were predictors of good outcome at discharge. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2018 APA, all rights reserved)
