AB  - A growing body of evidence links diabetes, insulin resistance and lack of physical activity (PA) as risk factors for and poor prognostic indicators of cancer survivorship, particularly among African American (AA) women who bear a disproportionate burden of diabetes. Our objective was to gather information from AA women with diabetes who are also breast or endometrial cancer survivors, on perceived benefits of and barriers to PA and preferred components for a multi-faceted PA intervention. Trained interviewers conducted semi-structured interviews with 20 AA cancer survivors (14 breast; 6 endometrial) with diabetes. Open-ended questions were posed to stimulate discussion and interviews were audio recorded and transcribed verbatim. Two investigators independently reviewed and coded each transcript to identify major themes using Atlas.ti v7. The median age of participants was 62 years; 30% were Medicare/Medicaid recipients. Survivors identified 6 key themes to express perceived barriers and preferred components for a PA intervention to promote glucose control and survivorship: (1) PA program should include face-to-face interaction (e.g., group, in-person counseling) with web support; (2) exercise routines should be cognizant of diabetic survivors' comorbidities; (3) it is important to partner with other PA program participants who are knowledgeable about diabetes and cancer survivorship; (4) program leaders should be aware that mental health is a major issue that is not always addressed by the survivors' primary care teams; (5) diabetic survivors rely on familiar resources for information on exercise (e.g., physician guidance, cancer support groups, cancer websites) and (6) diabetic survivors have a strong motivation to make healthy behavior changes, but expressed the need for additional support to achieve their PA and lifestyle goals. This formative data will inform the development of a pilot intervention and future testing in a randomized trial.
