AB  - All definitions of the need for protective service should include 2 components: defective mental functioning & defective soc resources. An estimate of 7% would put the number of mentally impaired older persons in need of protective service in the US today at about 1.5 million. The pop studied here is part of the Benjamin Rose Instit (RI) service project which concentrated on the noninstit'ized older person who was likely to come to the attention of welfare & health agencies. An analysis of the demographic characteristics of the survey persons & the 164 persons who constituted the demonstration sample suggests that these old people needing protective services are predominantly over 75, F, white, native born & nonmarried. They have only a grammar Sch educ, live in their own homes, & live on soc security or old age assistance, with incomes rarely more than $150 & often less than $100 a month. The cases were randomly allocated to 2 groups as they were referred: the exp'al or sample group (N=76), & the control or standard treatment group (N=88). All R's were interviewed by res staff during the demonstration yr. With the exp'al S's, instead of intervening largely through verbal interaction it was considered necessary to enlist environmental supports to reach the client, restructuring the situation to compensate for his deficiencies. To assess change & evaluate the effectiveness of the service, 4 aspects of the participants' lives & situations were measured: competence, environmental protection, affect, & effect on others. Survival & instit'ization were also examined. It was found mainly that participants in the exp'al service program were instit'ized earlier than they otherwise would have been, & that this instit'ization did not, contrary to intent, prove protective of the older persons although it did relieve collaterals & community agents. E. Weiman.
