AB  - On March I, 1934 in Anniston, Alabama, Benjamin and Myrtle Wright were given the gift of life. They named their manchild Bobby E. Wright. Their son grew and developed to become one of the most brilliant and dedicated men of his time. Bobby earned both a Bachelor of Science degree in Education and a Master of Science in Counseling and Guidance from Chicago State University as well as the Doctor of Philosophy Degree in Clinical Psychology from the University of Chicago. Encouraged and inspired by his wife, Easter, and motivated by the will to create a new world for his son Marcus Delaney, Bobby E. Wright's professional career included many dimensions. He was a truant officer, a teacher, college educator, school psychologist, mental health administrator, clinical psychologist, researcher, consultant, author, and political activist. Bobby's sense of commitment was equaled only by his sense of excellence. In recogniz- ing that the blood of our ancestors required no less than the best he had to of- fer, Dr. Wright gave to the cause of Black liberation his complete life force. He tirelessly gave of his knowledge and skills. As a pillar in the Chicago com- munity, Bobby was recognized and respected nationally and internationally for his intellectual ability, technical skills, and commitment to the progress of Black people. He was sought after and provided advice, guidance, and direc- tions to educational institutions, hospitals, community organizations, mental health agencies, national and international professional associations, and community and student groups throughout the country. From 1973 to 1981 Bobby served as the Director of the Garfield Park Comprehensive Mental Health Center, the largest Black comprehensive men- tal health center in the nation. While at Garfield Park, Bobby guided the trans- formation of the center. He literally changed the center from a one program operation serving a limited number of clients in a sheltered workshop setting to a strong independent freestanding Black controlled and operated comprehen- sive mental health center with an annual budget of over 2.5 million dollars and serving some 56,000 persons annually. As an authentic Black intellectual, Bobby Wright worked and struggled in the name of Black people on many fronts. He was a member of and belonged to the Association of Black Psychologists (Chicago Chapter), The Conference of Minority Public Administrators, National Alliance for Black School Educa- tors, The Society of Clinical and Experimental Hypnosis, Communiversity, Blyden Center for Creative Development, African Community of Chicago, Chicago and National Black United Front (health committee), Political Action Conference of Illinois, Science and Technology Commission, Sixth Pan- African Conference-Tanzania (chair), Charter Commission, National Black
