AB  - Background: Respect for autonomy is key in biomedical ethics and a particularly vulnerable principle in everyday medical practice [1]. Autonomy is strongly associated with the idea that patients should be allowed and enabled to make their own decisions about treatments for their diseases they will receive with purpose, substantial understanding and freedom from controlling influences [2]. In psychiatry, paternalism tends to be the dominant perspective among psychiatric staff members despite awareness of patients' right to autonomy and of their duty for reciprocity; that is building trust between staff and patients and involving patients in the planning and implementation of their care [3]. Aim(s): The aim of the present review is to summarize the evidence on the capacity of people with schizophrenia or bipolar disorder to make decisions about their healthcare and treatment and for stating their preferences for the management of their disease in real life. Building up evidence to recognise schizophrenia and bipolar disorder people capacity to make decisions about their treatments is the first step in the consideration of their autonomy. Method(s): To achieve the review objectives, a systematic search of the literature for articles published in English up to 31st December 2018 was conducted in PubMed and Scopus. The terms used for searching the databases included psychosis, schizophrenia, bipolar disorder, treatment and medication, decision making, preferences, consent, advance directives, capacity. Citation lists of relevant publications were hand-searched. Result(s): Searches identified a total of 1,337 hits. 92 potentially relevant papers were retained. Of these, 56 were excluded after assessing the full texts and 36 were finally included: 22 examined the capacity to make decisions regarding treatment and consent to treatment, and 14 the capacity to state preferences and make advance directives. Findings showed that more than 70% of schizophrenia and schizoaffective disorder outpatients understood the relevance of their disease and treatment at the point of making decisions about their healthcare. Furthermore, schizophrenia and bipolar disorder patients may be as competent in making decisions about their treatments in everyday life as non-psychiatric individuals. Patients treated voluntarily had considerably better scores for decisional capacity than those treated involuntarily. Decision-making capacity improved over time from admission to discharge amongst psychiatry inpatients. Schizophrenia patients were able to meaningfully complete conjoint analysis tasks and to base their choices for better treatment scenarios on their own preferences. Psychotic disorder patients were able to make treatment plans and to declare their preferred options related to medications. Apart from considering variations in capacity over time, decisional capacity judgements should be based on the type of decision to be made; the severity of the acute episode, and the specific phase of the mental disorder. Conclusion(s): The studies assessed reflect the capacity of schizophrenia and bipolar individuals in everyday life and indicate that the proportion of individuals with no capacity for making decisions on their treatments is very small. Therefore, the majority of patients with schizophrenia or bipolar disorder are capable of decision making and should be involved in decisions about the care of their health. Disclosure statement: This abstract is financially supported by an educational grant from Ferrer InternacionalCopyright © 2020
