AB  - Background: HCV eradication in injecting drug users (PWID) can be reached with Direct-Acting-Antivirals (DAA), and has been investigated in active injectors and those treated with opioid agonist therapy (OAT), but never in those recovering from addiction through a residential drug free approach We investigated the feasibility of HCV-eradication in the context of a drug free Therapeutic Community (San Patrignano, Italy) Methods: All PWID admitted during the DAA-era (2015- 2019) were enrolled Data on HCV-Antibody (HCVAb) and ribonucleic acid (HCV-RNA) testing, care and treatment were used to populate the HCV cascade (Fig 2) Results: 652 PWID (74 9% males, median age 29 years) were evaluated Among them 648 (99 4%) were screened for HCV infection at entry 423 subjects (65% overall) resulted HCVAb-pos, of which 266 individuals already tested HCVAb-positive pre-TC entry, while 157 (37 1%) newly diagnosed (Fig 1) 414/423 (97 9%) were screened for HCV-RNA of which 293 (70 8%) resulted viraemic DAA treatment has been prescribed in 235 (80 2%) while six (2 0%) are still on waiting list because foreign SVR was obtained in 220/224 (98 2%) subjects with suitable follow up, whereas 11 are still on treatment Four individuals were not cured: 1 suspension (rash linked to ribavirine), 1 death and 1 drop out before end-treatment, and 1 not-respondent No HCV-reinfection was observed Reason for not treatment in the remaining 52 viraemic subjects (17 8%) were: 30 discharged from TC before HCV-treatment, 15 dropout, 2 exitus, 2 refusals, 2 with advanced disease and 1 jailed Conclusion(s): HCV screening in PWID is still insufficient: even in DAA era one-third are unaware of being HCVAb-pos Once identified HCVAb-pos individuals are almost universally linked to care and >80% initiated DAA-treatment with virtually complete cure Major drawback is the drop-out, especially in the early phase of the study when DAA could be prescribed only in patients with advanced stage fibrosis; but modification of redeemability criteria will lead to a progressive improvement of the rate of treatment Environments like TC, Rehab centres and jail, can be considered optimal points of care for HCV eradication Treating both addiction and HCV reduces the risk of HCV re-infection among PWID toward zero.
