AB  - Over a two-year period, we assessed the effectiveness of a treatment program—Stop Now and Plan (SNAP™)—which is designed for children suffering with behavioural problems. The program was adapted to meet the needs of a Community Mental Health Center in Northern Ontario, which offers services to children living in rural and low population density areas. When the study began at the end of the first year of the program administration, the first group of children and their parents had received the 12 sessions and the individualized components. Our goal was first to review these participant files and to analyze pre and post intervention measures of problem behaviors as a test of program efficacy. These review and statistical analyses were also performed to assess the quality and psychometric properties of the clinical tools chosen to measure the program outcome and to identify other instruments that should be administered to the next group of children. As a result of the analyses performed at the end of the first year of the program administration, a number of other research instruments were identified for use during the second year of the program. Clinical measures were administered at that time (described in this chapter); pre and post intervention for eight participant groups. Results showed that about two thirds of participants achieved treatment goals. The effectiveness of both types of programs (individual or group) was measured for children exhibiting severe and comorbid disorders (conduct disorder, attention deficit hyperactivity disorder, anxiety or depression). The program reduced externalizing and internalizing symptoms and improved social adjustment. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
