AB  - The article presents information on group therapy and low-income Mexican Americans. Group therapy, to be effective with low-income Mexican Americans, must consider their specific psychological dysfunctions as well as their poverty and bicultural identification. Being a member of the Mexican American culture implies cultural duality, that is to say, "Mex leanness" as well as "Americanness." The full implications of this cultural duality cannot be simplistically specified because of the great variance in cultural identification among natives of different regions of Mexico. In addition to the duality of the Mexican American culture, understanding this culture is complicated by the regional. generational and socioeconomic variants of its dynamic and heterogeneous qualities. Members of the group usually attend consistently, adhere to requirements about confidentiality, achieve meaningful group participation and show marked improvement. As put into effect, the group approach has been friendly, directive, practical and behavioral. Members are encouraged to discuss their concerns, listen, give support to each other, use problem-solving techniques, role play, behave more assertively, increase social and job-seeking skills and appreciate humor.
