AB  - This article presents an experimental pretest-posttest treatment control study which examines the effectiveness of group coping skills training in reducing relapse in persons with schizophrenia. The course of schizophrenia fluctuates greatly and is characterized by frequent relapse, exacerbation of illness and limitations in psychosocial functioning. Although social work has provided leadership in the development and evaluation of psychosocial models of treatment of schizophrenia in the areas of community support programs, the development of models of social work practice and evaluation of patients in group work, with schizophrenics is limited. There is growing commitment to the design of psychosocial treatments in mental health practice that are grounded in knowledge of the psychopathology of specific diagnostic groups. This disease is exacerbated by events in life which are stressful, interactions with family members which are emotionally draining and overstimulating treatment environments.
