AB  - Current theories of schizophrenia emphasize the role of neurological malfunction and developmental disturbance as key etiological factors associated with disabilities. The purposes of this research were: (1) To test the effectiveness of a social skills training program that employed traditional teaching/learning methods as part of an overall outpatient program, (2) To test the effectiveness of incorporating a computer-assisted problem solving program into a social skills intervention, and (3) To test the utility of the Stress-Vulnerability-Coping-Competence Model for guiding treatment decisions and predicting rehabilitation outcomes. An experimental design was used to observe the effects of a social skills problem solving program with or without incorporating a computer program to remediate and/or compensate for the deficits in problem solving skills typical of some clients with schizophrenia. Pre- and post-test measures included Demographic data, the Brief Psychiatric Rating Scale (BPRS) (Overall & Gorham, 1962), the Schedule of Negative Symptoms (SANS) (Andreasen, 1982), the Social Problem Solving Inventory-R (SPSI-R) (D'Zurilla & Nezu, 1980), the Index of Clinical Stress ( ICS) (Abell, 1992), the Ways of Coping Scale ( WAYS) (Folkman & Lazarus, 1988) and the Social Competence Inventory (SCI) (Psychiatric Rehabilitation Consultants, 1997). The original data analysis plan to control for level of education and program site was not implemented since these extraneous variables did not impact on mediators or outcomes. Hypothesis 1 predicted that the computer group would demonstrate more improvement in regression-adjusted change scores than the board games group. No significant differences in change scores were detected with regard to psychiatric symptomatology, social functioning or stress. Hypothesis 2 predicted that the combined experimental groups would demonstrate more improvement on regression-adjusted change scores than would the control group. Only five control subjects completed the study, making comparisons between the control group and the experimental groups highly unstable and unreliable. The Therapeutic Group Intervention Factors, ( TGIF) was completed for each group session. Review of group notes suggested a concern that the manipulation of the independent variable was somewhat diluted due to small group size, inconsistent group attendance and the inability of some members to generate problems for group discussion. This investigation was seriously underpowered due to the unanticipated small sample. Uneven attrition from the control group rendered comparisons with the combined experimental groups highly unstable and unreliable. Even with these threats to internal validity, this investigation produced encouraging support for continued query. (Abstract shortened by UMI.) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
