AB  - Dual diagnosis patients face a range of barriers to substance abuse treatment. The term dual diagnosis is used here to describe the coexistence of a mental health disorder and an alcohol or drug use disorder. In addition, dual diagnosis patients may have characteristics and traits that hinder their full participation in outpatient group therapy, limiting their ability to successfully engage and complete treatment. Recent findings in retention studies, however, suggest that dual diagnosis outpatients do not differ in their treatment experiences from substance abuse outpatients. The objective of this paper is to determine if the presence of a dual diagnosis is a significant determinant of retention in treatment among Medicaid financed, adult substance abuse outpatients. The setting of the study was a single addiction treatment program serving medically underserved communities in Chicago, Illinois. 82% of outpatients were of African descent and 54% were male. The utilization records of 437 outpatients were analyzed using an administrative data set, including 83 dual diagnosis patients and 354 substance use disorder patients. Evaluation endpoints included the utilization of group therapy visits and the completion of the primary phase of Intensive Outpatient Treatment (IOT). Analytic procedures included descriptive statistics, survival methods and regression analyses. Dual diagnosis patients averaged 12.3 group therapy visits compared to 16.6 group therapy visits for substance use disorder patients. Without statistical adjustment, the odds were that dual diagnosis patients were 1.8 times more likely to not complete IOT primary care than patients with only diagnoses of substance use disorder (crude odds ratio of 1.82, Chi-square 4.21, p = .04). However, the effect of the presence of a dual diagnosis was not significant when adjusting for age, race, sex, and enrollment after welfare reform (January 1, 1997). Age, race and enrollment after welfare reform were important confounding variables and consistently altered the association between dual diagnosis and retention. The data of this small study suggest that substance abuse clinicians and administrators should be optimistic about the prognosis of dual diagnosis patients in outpatient substance abuse treatment. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
