AB  - In 2011, the author began working as a case manager at an East Harlem supportive housing site for adults with mental illness who have experienced homelessness. He had never worked with underserved adults, and he quickly noticed that the majority of tenants experienced obesity and related issues, such as hypertension, diabetes, asthma, and joint problems. Indeed, almost every client experienced a combination of those chronic, preventable health conditions. A year later, he started working at another supportive housing site, where he observed the same health issues among tenants. He later learned that people with mental illness die, on average, 25 years earlier than the general population, primarily resulting from those same chronic health problems. With more experience, he frequently noted the connection between physical and mental health.
