AB  - Developed and evaluated an 8‐wk functional fitness strength training program using dumbbells and ankle weights for 16 residents (aged 71‐96 yrs) of a multi‐level assisted living facility in Texas. Functional performance (FP), strength and activities of daily living tests were taken at baseline and at the end of 8 wks. Weekly 30‐min upper and lower body exercises with progressively weighted dumbbells (1‐4 lbs) and ankle weights (starting at 1 lb) was for treatment group 1 while group 2 used 1‐lb dumbbells and ankle straps with no weight for the 8‐wk duration. Significant gains in muscular strength and FP was measured in group 1 while improvement in FP was seen in group 2. Ss reported benefits in socialization as a result of enhanced daily function, which is seen as a way of preventing depression in this at‐risk population. Results indicate that the program is a feasible and cost‐effective method of improving quality of life and preventing depression in older adults. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2009 APA, all rights reserved)
