AB  - HIV, hepatitis B & C, drug overdose, & other drug-related health problems still pose significant health risks to injection drug users (IDUs) & their sexual partners, indicating the need for further development of innovative public health interventions. A relatively new intervention implemented in many municipalities throughout the world is the "safer injection site" (SIS). An SIS is a legal facility that allows people to prepare & inject pre-obtained drugs in a hygienic, anxiety-free atmosphere under the supervision of health personnel. This paper examines the responses of a sample of lDUs in New York City to whether they would use an SIS should one be implemented in mid-town Manhattan. The SIS would be part of a comprehensive harm reduction project that already offers needle-exchange, street outreach, testing & counseling, support groups, referral services, including drug treatment, & on-site primary medical & dental services. The results of our study indicate that a large majority of the IDUs sampled would use an SIS should one be implemented, & that those most likely to use it are lDUs at the highest risk for contracting or spreading blood-borne diseases such as HIV & hepatitis, & for experiencing a drug overdose. 4 Tables, 29 References. Adapted from the source document.
