AB  - Examined the impact of a treatment process for substance users in relation to time spent in treatment. 248 patients (mean age 33.5 yrs) of a public rehabilitation center for alcoholics and drug addicts were tested before and 5 mo after treatment with the Addiction Severity Index (ASI). Exposure to treatment was based on the number of clients' contact hours with a therapist. The sample was divided into 3 groups according to number of hours spent in treatment: Group 1 spent 21 hrs or less in treatment, Group 2 spent between 22 and 47 hrs in treatment, and Group 3 spent more than 47 hrs in treatment. The data were analyzed using multivariate analysis of variance (MANOVA) on the seven scales of the ASI for the 3 groups and the 2 time periods. Results show that the severity of addiction problems decreased after treatment, and decreased more for Ss who underwent treatment for a longer period of time. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
