AB  - Reports an error in the original article by G. Broe et al (International Journal of Geriatric Psychiatry, 1998 [Oct], Vol 13[10], 667–673). The scale score used to measure depression based on the 20-item CES-D instrument was incorrectly computed. Corrections are made to the scale score, and the impact of these alterations on the findings are discussed. (The following abstract of this article originally appeared in record [rid]1998-11246-002[/rid].) Examined the effects of a range of chronic systemic and neurological disorders on 3 life quality indicators: disability, depressive symptoms, and life satisfaction. As part of the Sydney Older Persons Study, a community survey was carried out with 434 non-demented people aged 75+ yrs living in Sydney, Australia. Ss were given a medical examination covering the following disorders: heart disease, chronic lung disease, bone and joint disease, stroke, visual loss, peripheral vascular disease, obesity, other systemic diseases, gait ataxia, gait slowing (including Parkinsonism) and cognitive impairment short of dementia. They were also assessed on a clinician-rated disability scale and given self-report depression and life satisfaction scales. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
