AB  - The value of this study is significant as $60 billion was spent on gambling in 1997 compared with $47.2 billion on all other forms of entertainment and recreation. The National Gambling Impact Study Commission, June 1999 report findings included: 2.5% of Americans account for 15% of all betting, 1.5 million Americans met the DSM-IV criteria for pathological gambling and 15 million Americans have gambling problems. In summary, legalized gambling has expanded both in social acceptance and accessibility, as casinos now operate in 27 states. There have been no previous co-occurrence studies of the gender differences in depression, anxiety, substance abuse and gambling disorders comparing social and pathological gamblers. The main goal of this study was to investigate the gender differences in mental health (specifically anxiety and depression) and substance abuse disorders as predictors of gambling disorders. The research design for the study was a non-random, case correlational study. The research study questionnaire was comprised of several standardized instruments including: the Beck Anxiety Inventory (BAI), the Beck Depression Inventory (BDI), the Drug Abuse Screening Test (DAST), the AUDIT (Alcohol Use Disorders Identification Test), and the NODS (National Opinion Research Center DSM-IV Screen for Gambling Problems). The overall study sample consisted of two hundred people: 50 women and 50 men in gambling treatment (met DSM-IV criteria for pathological gambling) and 50 women and 50 men who were social gamblers (did not meet criteria for pathological gambling). Among the study results were women in the gambling treatment group had the highest rates of anxiety and depression. However, males in the community group had the highest rates of drug problems contrasted with males in the treatment group had the highest rates of alcohol problems. Age, employment status and depression were the predictive factors for females and depression and drug problems were the predictive factors for males. The findings of this study have implications for prevention, education, identification and intervention efforts for persons with gambling disorders. A related ramification is the need for mental health and substance treatment staff to screen for gambling disorders among all patients. Social workers as the largest providers of outpatient mental health services need training on identification, assessment and treatment of gambling problems.
