AB  - Interviews with nine female psychologists were analyzed utilizing qualitative research methodology to identify treatment issues for female survivors of childhood sexual abuse (CSA) with histories of alcoholism. The four key treatment factors which were identified by the therapists were providing for clients' physical and psychological safety, avoiding past mistakes, applying conditions before referring to Alcoholics Anonymous (AA), and enhancing client empowerment. Clinical implications for the dual treatment population included safety, boundaries, timing of therapy, empowerment, and referral to AA. Safety is a major concern in the clinical management of the CSA survivor with a history of alcoholism. Foremost in the safety protocol is the maintenance of sobriety. A second clinical factor concerns the prevention of blurred boundaries in the therapist-client relationship. With this client population there is a pull on the therapist toward over identification with clients and an urge to rescue. Timing is a significant clinical issue in that therapists have the tendency to encourage the clients to move through the therapy process too quickly in attempts to 'fix' clients or ease the clients' pain. Client empowerment is a major treatment outcome which is necessary to move clients from victim role to survivor role. The focal point for clients to reclaim personal power is the development of self-advocacy in current situations and relationships. Additionally, AA can be effectively used as a support group for female dual treatment clients only after certain conditions are met. These conditions include (a) client preparation concerning surrender and helplessness as related only to the alcohol, not related to self, (b) use of women only groups, and (c) non use of male sponsors. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
