AB  - Describes how a day treatment program in an inner-city hospital approached the treatment of the limited-functioning chronically mentally ill (LFCMI) through innovative group psychotherapy techniques. Patients participating in this program were typically socioeconomically deprived with diagnoses of schizophrenia, organic brain syndrome, and major depression. The cases of 2 male patients (aged 21 and 61 yrs) indicate that LFCMI patients can display both positive and negative symptoms. LFCMI patients were grouped so that a therapist could focus on their specific needs, addressing deficits that impede even limited community acceptance of this population. The role of the group therapist and the techniques used are elaborated. The indication of the success of this approach was the advancement of LFCMI to a higher level of the program. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
