AB  - Evaluated the applicability of R. M. Schwartz and G. L. Garamoni's (see PA, Vol 76:36686) states of mind (SOM) model for assessing the influence of cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) on cognitive functioning of social phobics. 11 female and 19 male social phobics receiving either CBT or educational‐supportive group treatment were compared for SOM change. Ss in both treatments evidenced negative monolog (a preponderance of negative thoughts) at pretreatment. Although the groups did not differ at posttreatment, CBT Ss reached positive dialog at a 6‐mo follow‐up, while the others did not. Findings extend previous research by showing that a treatment that explicitly emphasizes cognitive aspects of anxiety and cognitive coping responses could sustain and augment increased positive thinking at follow‐up.
