AB  - Objective: 1) Determine the effect of opioid-specific counseling on post-discharge opioid consumption after pelvic organ prolapse (POP) surgery. 2) Evaluate the effect of opioid-specific counseling on postoperative opioid storage/disposal patterns. 3) Assess the rate of additional opioid prescriptions for postoperative pain within 6 weeks of surgery. Method(s): This is a multi-center randomized controlled trial of subjects undergoing POP surgery at an academic medical center and community HMO between December 2017 and May 2019. Subjects were randomized to either standard (prescription of postoperative analgesics with general counseling) or opioid-specific perioperative counseling. Opioid-specific counseling was provided at the preoperative and 2-week postoperative visits and included educational handouts with information about the risks of opioid consumption and FDA instructions for appropriate opioid storage and disposal. All subjects were prescribed a uniform analgesic regimen including ibuprofen, acetaminophen, and 150 morphine milligram equivalents (MME) of oxycodone. Outcomes including subject-reported post-discharge MME consumption, frequency of appropriate opioid storage and disposal, and number of additional opioid prescriptions were assessed 6 weeks postoperatively. Result(s): Among 126 subjects (68 opioid-specific and 58 standard counseling), there were no significant differences in demographic or clinical characteristics, type of POP surgery, concomitant hysterectomy or anti-incontinence procedure, length of hospitalization, or perioperative complications between subjects in the 2 arms (all P>0.05). For the primary outcome, there was no significant difference in median post-discharge MME consumption between groups (22.5mg for opioidspecific, 4.4mg for standard, P>0.05). Median (IQR) post-discharge MME consumption for the 126 patients was 14.3mg (0 mg,75 mg), which is equivalent to 2 tablets of PO oxycodone 5mg. 52 (41.3%) subjects denied post-discharge opioid consumption. For secondary outcomes, there were no significant differences in appropriate opioid storage patterns between groups. The overall disposal rate of unused opioid tablets was higher among subjects who received opioid-specific counseling (61.8% for opioid-specific, 36.2% for standard, P<0.01), but the rate of appropriate opioid disposal did not significantly differ between groups (92.7% for opioid-specific, 95.2% for standard, P>0.05). Rates of additional opioid prescriptions for postoperative pain were similar between groups (7.1% for opioid-specific, 7.7% for standard, P>0.05). In secondary analysis, HMO subjects had access to a medicine drop box within the facility and this disposal method was utilized more often among HMO subjects compared to academic subjects (23.1% for HMO, 1.4% for academia, p<0.05). Conclusion(s): Opioid-specific preoperative counseling did not impact postoperative opioid consumption but did increase the overall disposal rate of unused opioid tablets 6 weeks postoperatively. Providing opioid-specific preoperative counseling could minimize the potential for opioid misuse by reducing the number of unused opioid tablets remaining after surgery. Availability of a healthcare facility drop box increases patient utilization of this particular disposal method.
