AB  - ; The article presents information on a randomized, double-blind trial which investigated the effectiveness of sustained-release bupropion as compared with placebo. Subjects maintained a daily diary to record smoking rates and symptoms of nicotine withdrawal and completed the Fagerstrom Tolerance Questionnaire and Beck Depression Inventory. Self-reported abstinence was validated by carbon monoxide measurement in expired air at each visit. Intention-to-treat analysis was used and subjects missing a follow-up visit were considered to be smoking. The results of the study many not be applicable to the typical family practice office. The degree of counseling and follow-up used in this study, which may be as important as the drug therapy, may not be practical in a busy primary care setting. The primary outcome was the rate of smoking cessation at different times for the four treatment groups. Efficacy of smoking cessation was evaluated and reported in two ways, namely weekly rates and rates of continuous abstinence. The results of the study reported as point-prevalence rates are not comparable with those of other studies of pharmacologic smoking interventions that typically reported results as confirmed continuous abstinence.
