AB  - Evaluated the effectiveness of the Group Competency Program (GCP), which is designed to enhance the group competencies of clinical staff working at a state psychiatric hospital. Staff group facilitators at a state psychiatric hospital met weekly to monitor their progress in implementing the GCP. 40 inpatients (aged 12-57 yrs) diagnosed with schizophrenia, schizoaffective disorder, bipolar disorder, major depression, posttraumatic stress disorder, oppositional defiant disorder, reactive attachment disorder, or obsessive-compulsive disorder completed the GCP intervention during a 6-mo period. Patient subjects (Ss) completed questionnaires at baseline and at multiple follow-ups concerning comfort in a group, interpersonal relations, social role performance, anxiety/depression, somatic symptoms, social problems, behavioral dysfunction, group cohesion, self-disclosure, cognitive understanding, inter-member distrust and conflict, and satisfaction with group treatment. Results show that Ss' satisfaction with group treatment was greatest during the middle portion of the intervention. Adolescent Ss reported little involvement with their group in the initial stages, but significantly improved 4 mo subsequently. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
