AB  - Objective: To investigate the role of pharmacists in improving adherence to injectable extended-release naltrexone (Vivitrol) therapy by comparing the number of patients receiving greater than six months of Vivitrol therapy when managed by a pharmacist-led Vivitrol program at a community pharmacy as compared to an outpatient primary care clinic. Method(s): A retrospective review will be performed of patients 18 years and older receiving once-monthly Vivitrol therapy from January 1, 2017 through December 31, 2017 at St. Matthews Community Pharmacy (SCMP) and an outpatient primary care clinic in Louisville, KY. The Vivitrol administered at the out-patient primary care clinic is dispensed by SMCP. Patients presenting to SMCP for enrollment in the Vivitrol program may already have a prescription or be prescribed therapy through referral to a dispensing physician. Prescription processing, a urine drug screen, educational counseling, and a test dose of naltrexone are all provided at the initial visit by a staff pharmacist. Following this visit, the pharmacy tracks adherence and follows up with the patient two to three days prior to the next dose. Administration and education for subsequent doses are performed by staff pharmacists at the pharmacy. The data collected in this study will include the indication for Vivitrol (opioid dependence or alcohol dependence), previous treatment with naltrexone therapies, and the number of continuous injections received at SMCP through the pharmacist led program or out-patient primary care site. This study has been submitted to Sullivan University's Institutional Review Board for approval. Result(s): Research in progress. Conclusion(s): Pharmacists have a reputation of improving adherence in special populations and increasing access to care through the implementation of referral protocols. Patients receiving extended release injectable Vivitrol may experience reduced relapse occurrence; however, some patients lack thoroughly involved therapy management, making adherence to Vivitrol therapy an issue. This study may potentially demonstrate pharmacists' role in improving adherence to Vivitrol injection therapy.
