Y1  - 2009///
AB  - Children living in violent communities are being traumatized by continual exposure to shootings, stabbings, fights, arrests, domestic violence, and violent media. To date, the majority of research on the effects of exposure to community violence has focused on school-age children and adolescents. While few studies have examined the prevalence of community violence exposure in populations of very young children, or how such exposure correlates to behavior and development, initial findings indicate that preschoolers are being exposed to disturbingly high rates of community violence similar to that of their school-age and adolescent counterparts, and a need has been identified for interventions with young children exposed to such violence. The purpose of this dissertation is to foster understanding regarding preschoolers' exposure to community violence; explore how preschoolers' perceive violence and how exposure to violence impacts their development; and to identify age appropriate strategies for talking with young children about community violence. This study integrates research findings regarding the prevalence and effects of exposure to community violence on young children, current theories regarding early childhood development, and treatment interventions designed for school-age children in order to create a psychoeducational manual for preschoolers, parents, and teachers. The manual consists of psychoeducation for parents and teachers on the topics of posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD); preschool children's cognitive and social development; and the impact of exposure to community violence on preschool children's development. In addition, the manual outlines a six week, school-based, group intervention for preschoolers (ages 4 to 6) on the topics of community; community violence; feelings; grief and loss; and safety and coping. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
