AB  - Addiction is a pressing issue that is impacting health, mental wellbeing, health care costs, economic spending, overdoses, and family dynamics. The family members of people with addiction are suffering from increased levels of stress and have limited services to seek support. Long-term stress has been found to increase the risk of developing medical conditions and comorbidities, disrupt mental health, and reduce productivity and lifespan. There are limited research studies that address stress-reducing interventions for family members of individuals with addiction. This clinical scholarly project (CSP) presents the results of implementing a five-week-long virtual support group to improve perceived stress in family members of individuals with addiction. A sample of 13 men and women in Clark County, Nevada who are family members of people that are dealing with substance abuse participated in the pre/post-study. Participation of the sample was subjected to the collection of the Perceived Stress Scale (PSS) results before the virtual support group began and after the group concluded. A paired-samples t-test analyzed the results, which revealed that the intervention had statistically significant results on decreasing stress. On average, the PSS score was 8.00 points lower after the intervention, t(12) = 4.00, p = .002, 95% CI [3.62, 12.39]. Implications are considered for future research to better understand the practice of virtual support groups in reducing perceived stress. The findings from this study suggest that virtual support groups are effective in decreasing perceived stress in individuals of family members with addiction. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
