AB  - Criminal recidivism was assessed for 10 highly disruptive & aggressive, incarcerated juvenile offenders who received decompression treatment, for 10 matched controls who received mental health treatment services, & for 10 assessment-only controls. Treated participants were matched with controls on a number of demographic, behavioral, & personality variables. Decompression treatment was developed to improve the institutional adjustment of mentally disordered adult offenders & is adapted here for juvenile offenders. Participants in the decompression treatment & usual treatment groups were both significantly less likely to recidivate in the approximately 2-year follow-up period than were assessment-only subjects who received mental health assessment only & received the usual juvenile corrections rehabilitation services (10%, 20%, & 70% recidivism, respectively). The results suggest that decompression treatment may be successfully applied to the most unmanageable & disruptive institutionalized juvenile offenders. 1 Table, 1 Figure, 13 References. Adapted from the source document.
