AB  - This chapter will focus on discrimination about psychosis and outline the importance of stigmatized representations of psychosis and their implications for public health and clinical services. Discrimination is a major concern for service users. It has a variety of negative consequences including the prevention of help seeking, increasing the duration of untreated psychosis (DUP), decreasing self-esteem and increasing social anxiety. The chapter will describe how community-based interventions focused on the reduction of discrimination can facilitate change in public attitudes, promoting early detection and intervention for individuals experiencing psychosis and maximizing the likelihood of recovery. It will also describe individual and group interventions to address negative consequences of discrimination. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
