AB  - The implementation of school based mental health programs promises to increase student academic achievement and decrease student violence. However, it is unclear whether or not these programs fulfill these promises. This research study was conducted at an urban-fringe elementary school located in New Jersey that received a federal grant to create a School Based Mental Health (SBMH) Program. The Counseling and Intervention Program (CIP) offered therapy to any student in the school. The CIP was not held to the stringent standards of the Individuals with Disabilities Improvement Education Act (IDIEA) because it operated separately from the Child Study Team (CST). The theoretical framework for this research centered on the Social Learning Theory (SLT). The components of the Social Learning Theory indicated that CIP services should have resulted academic and behavioral improvement by the participating students. This study compared the year before (2007) and the year after (2008) the implementation of Counseling and Intervention Program. The students were matched to themselves across the two years, to see if the intervention was associated with increases or decreases in student performance in several categories. The research questions sought to answer whether or not the students that received CIP treatment, increased academically and decreased violent behaviors. The CIP was comprised of 4 licensed and credentialed mental health professionals. The study population consisted of the 346 elementary school students that were referred for CIP services. The students that were given parental consent received services (treatment group). The students that did not receive parental consent did not receive services (control group). This quasi-experimental study utilized a pretest-posttest with control group design. Descriptive statistics were used to summarize the raw data after it was collected. A Mixed ANCOVA and independent and paired t-tests were used to compare the treatment and control groups. This design was an analysis plan with parallel execution that analyzed the participating general and special education students separately. The analysis proposed two components; the first was a between-group comparison (treatment versus control) which tested for the main effect of group. The test for the main effect of group was the most important component for determining statistical significance of the treatment. The second component was a within-group comparison, which tested for the main effect of time. This produced a linear relationship producing three possible results of the treatment: positive effect, no effect, or negative effect. The results of the analysis were interpreted at the p<.05 level of significance. Interpretation of the statistics indicated that one of the four null hypotheses should be rejected in favor of the alternative hypotheses. The other three null hypotheses were retained. The research yielded results that showed statistical significance regarding the implementation of the CIP for decreased student violence by the general education students that received treatment. The answers to the research questions of this research are that there was only an effect on the general education students regarding behavioral performance when a school administration implements a school based mental health program. There was no effect when a school administration implemented a mental health program for general education students regarding academic performance and special education students with regards to increased academic success and decreased student violence. In the area of general education behavioral performance, this research showed effect on the treatment group above and beyond the covariates of gender, ethnicity, socioeconomic status, and grade level. The covariates of gender, socioeconomic status and grade level also showed significance in different outcome areas of the academic… (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
