AB  - While the working alliance in psychotherapy is predictive of treatment outcome, some vulnerable populations do not readily establish such a relationship. Studied here is the effect of a specific social support service on the working alliances established by poor, mentally-ill mothers. The study investigated the hypothesis that the provision of in-home family support services would positively affect the working alliances of mentally-ill mothers in psychotherapy. Sixty-seven mothers with chronic mental illness were randomly assigned to receive either psychotherapy or psychotherapy supplemented by a nine-month, in-home intervention. Measures of the working alliance (WAI-C) were administered before the random selection of participants to the intervention or comparison group and again at the completion of the intervention. Two level ANOVAs showed no differences in the groups at post-test. Results indicated that the provision of in-home family support intervention provided concurrently with psychotherapy for nine months had no systematic effect on the working alliance in psychotherapy. The study findings indicate that any benefits of a home-based service lie outside their impact on the mother's therapeutic alliance as studied here. The alliance itself, under these study conditions, was independent of the studied support service. The study revealed significant associations between the working alliance and the mothers' adaptive achievements during the intervention. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
