AB  - The currently used treatments are address mainly positive symptoms, which are most often kept under control by antipsychotic medication, leaving patients with numerous residual phenomena such as negative symptoms, cognitive decline, reduced social drive, symptoms which all lead to functional impairment and low quality of life (Aleman et al., 2017). The concept of quality of life was initially defined, by the World Health Organization (WHO), as the degree of satisfaction or a general feeling of well-being perceived by the individual regarding physical health, psychological state, level of independence, social relationships, and personal beliefs (WHOQOL Group, 1995), as being a subjective concept. Method Inclusion and exclusion criteria The study was conducted at the Outpatient Psychiatric Clinic, Cluj-Napoca, Romania. 87 consecutive patients who met the following inclusion criteria were included in the study: men and women with ages ranging between 18-60 years; patients met the ICD-10 diagnostic criteria for schizophrenia; patients were stable on treatment for the last three months and lived in a community setting; patients were excluded if they had other psychiatric comorbidities or debilitating chronic diseases. The collected data were age, gender, level of education, marital status, employment status, age of onset of the disease, duration of the disease, number of admissions in the hospital, and treatment reported as equivalents of Chlorpromazine (Woods, 2003).
