AB  - This is a report of what is understood to be a new method of using a breathalyser as part of a treatment strategy in patients with long-term alcohol abuse. Breathalysers are most commonly used by police forces as a means of determining whether a person is over the legal limit of alcohol in relation to drinking and driving offences. They are also used in health settings, predominantly in accident and emergency and psychiatric units, as a rapid means of determining alcohol status. Their use is often regarded as being somewhat controversial and punitive. Previous research has produced conflicting evidence about their usefulness.1-5 It would seem that this is due as much to the rather thoughtless policies surrounding their use in such settings as is due to their criminal connotation. Following the successful management of some patients with alcohol problems previously thought to be intractable in which the use of a breathalyser was an integral part of their treatment plan, the following two cases are presented to illustrate the method.
