AB  - The effects of a modified therapeutic community model on the behavioral and psychological functioning of individuals diagnosed with both substance abuse and psychiatric problems was examined to determine if the East Bay Community Recovery Project's (EBCRP) outpatient program is helpful. Twenty-five dual-diagnosed clients between the ages of 27 and 56 years old, with varying degree of psychiatric and substance abuse were studied to determine if EBCRP's program is effective in reducing the number of medical problems, drug/alcohol use, legal problems, family conflicts, and psychiatric symptoms while increasing educational/training skills and social relationships. The instruments used included the Addiction Severity Index (ASI), the Addiction Severity Index Follow-Up, and the Brief Symptom Inventory (BSI). The results showed a reduction in drug/alcohol use, legal problems, family conflicts, and psychiatric symptoms. Results also revealed an increase in social relationships. However, results did not show a significant increase in education or training skills, nor were significant reductions found in medical problems. This study has added support to the overall literature in showing the validity of a modified therapeutic community model as a treatment modality that can work effectively in an outpatient day treatment setting and with dual-diagnosed individuals. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
