AB  - Background: Lack of access to primary care or inadequate health insurance coverage leads many patients in the U.S. to seek the hospital emergency department (ED) for mental health services. A potential channel for redirecting many of these patients is Community Health Centers (CHCs), which treat a medically underserved population of nearly 30 million individuals. Whether CHCs are effective in preventing mental health ED visits is unknown. Aims of the Study: Nearly 90% of U.S. CHCs provide mental health services, yet the level varies widely across facilities and over time. We exploit this variation in order to investigate to what extent EDs serve as a substitute for patients who lack access to mental health care in CHCs. A contributory aim is to identify unique market areas serviced by CHCs. Method(s): We first develop a novel process for identifying CHCyear specific service areas based on patient origin zip codes. We then estimate panel data models of ED visits for mental health services using the correlated random effects (CRE) approach which parametrically models the dependence between unobserved heterogeneity and observed covariates. The setting is the 53 Federally Qualified Health Centers in Florida during 2012-2016. The dependent variable is the number of ED visits in a CHC's service area, drawn from the HCUP State Emergency Department Databases (SID) and identified according to ICD-9 and ICD-10 diagnosis codes. Key explanatory variables measure number of CHC mental health visits and number of CHC mental health visits per patient obtained from the Health Resources and Services Administration Uniform Data System. We control for several supply side and demand side factors drawing from public use databases. Result(s): CHC mental health visits grew by 40% between 2012 and 2016. Controlling for population, we observe a small negative effect of CHC mental health visits in explaining ED mental health visits: an increase in 100 CHC visits is associated with a decrease of 2.12 ED visits (p=0.04). CHC visit intensity is not statistically significant. The CRE estimator detected and controlled for correlation between unobserved heterogeneity and CHC visit intensity. Discussion(s): While statistically significant, our observed effect of CHC visits is small and likely translates to limited impact at current levels of integration. We note that the magnitude we observe represents a lower bound as the HCUP SID databases match to only about 80% of acute care hospitals. Results also may not generalize nationally; however, this single state study allows capture of ED utilization at all hospitals in the state and under any payer. Implications for Health Policies: Results suggest that policymakers explore more avenues for regional coordination strategies that align services between CHCs and local hospitals. These might involve better communication around utilization or joint participation in quality improvement and/or health promotion efforts. Implications for Further Research: Continued study of the question initially examined here is warranted as CHCs continue to provide more behavioral health care. Our method for defining CHC market areas also can be applied in examining other outcomes and in other geographic settings.
