AB  - Discusses factors contributing to risk for acquired immune deficiency syndrome (AIDS) among chronic patients at an urban community mental health center. Stereotypes of the mentally ill as asexual or neutered have had dangerous consequences, namely the absence of sex education and AIDS prevention as an integral part of treatment. To counter this neglect, an AIDS prevention program was developed in a large inner-city mental health center that serves primarily Black and Latino patients. Prevention efforts include individual risk assessments and individual and group psychoeducational interventions. Central to effective patient education are the distribution of condoms and concrete instruction in their use. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
