AB  - [...]in the 1970s the famous UK/USA study had shown that the high frequency of schizophrenia and low frequency of affective disorders among mental hospital admissions in New York compared to those in London were largely due to the different psychiatric background inuencing the practice of diagnosis (Cooper et al. 1971). According to such a new descriptive approach, the diagnosis of Major Depressive Disorder in the DSM-IV (APA 1994), the ofcial classication system of the American Psychiatric Association and the most widely used system worldwide from 1994 to 2013, did not take into account the environmental and relational contexts in which symptoms occur. [...]as well as in DSM-IV, in DSM-5, a market distress out of proportion to the severity of intensity of the stressor (APA 2013) is required for such a diagnosis, indicating the need for an aethiopathological factor, the individual vulnerability, that causes the abnormal reaction. Medical literature devotes an immense space to drug therapies even though meta-analyses consistently reveal that most of the response to antidepressant treatment can be obtained by placebo (Kirsch and Low 2013) and a recent systematic review on comparative efcacy of treatments and controls for depression concludes that antidepressants alone and psychotherapy alone are not signicantly different from alternative therapies or active intervention controls and the type of treatment offered 123 202 Cult Med Psychiatry (2015) 39:200203 is less important than involving depressed patients in an active therapeutic program (Khan et al. 2012).
