KW  - eppi-reviewer
PY  - 2002
DA  - 2002///
Y1  - 2002///
AB  - The purpose of this study was to determine the effects of Moral Education on convicted felony sexual offenders. Fifty‐eight men were selected from an outpatient sexual offender treatment center to be involved in this study. This group included both parole and probation populations. These participants were randomly assigned to either the moral education group plus traditional therapy or traditional sexual offender treatment only. Pretesting on all participants was conducted using the Defining Issues Test (Rest, 1999) to assess levels of moral reasoning, and the Hare Psychopathology Checklist (Hare, 1999) to assess psychopathic personality disorders. Demographic data was gathered at the time of the pretest. The treatment group was randomly divided into two groups to allow for smaller discussion groups. Treatment consisted of eight sessions of group moral dilemma discussions. The researcher conducted the counseling sessions, and each research group met for 90 minutes one time per week for eight weeks. At the studies conclusion all participants of the moral education groups and the traditional treatment groups post‐tested with the Defining Issues Test (Rest, 1999). The results indicate that the moral education groups post‐test results significantly differed from traditional treatment group pre‐test scores, the moral education group pre‐test scores, as well as traditional therapy group post‐test scores. This post‐test research group result indicates an elevation in moral reasoning of approximately one Kohlberg Stage, from the level of junior high school reasoning to that of later senior high school. The traditional treatment group, however, displayed no variation between pre‐test and post‐test scoring in regards to moral/ethical thinking. Those participants within the research group who were diagnosed as having psychopathic personality disorders (16%) did not benefit to any significant degree from the moral education curriculum, showing pre‐test and post‐test scores equivalent to the traditional therapy group, and to the research group pre‐test scores. Results of this study indicate that moral education does significantly impact the level of moral reasoning among non‐psychopathic convicted sexual offenders. That level of impact was significant at the .05 level, and affected 84% of these randomly selected sexual offenders. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2009 APA, all rights reserved)
