AB  - The behavioral adjustment of individuals with developmental disabilities may be influenced by intra-personal factors as well as the interaction of the person and their environment. The aims of deinstitutionalization include the provision of normative community environments in which adaptive skills and positive behavioral outcomes may be realized. This study examined the potential determinants of levels of behavioral adjustment and the use of psychotropic medications and other interventions for individuals with mental retardation over time and comparatively between institutional and community-based environments. These determinants were analyzed using the conceptual framework of Matson's bio-psycho-social theory by which targeted variables relating to dual diagnosis (mental illness and mental retardation) are viewed as interdependent. Matson's model considers biological, social, and psychological/developmental processes as the three major factors that account for emotional disorders. The study looks at outcomes of the deinstitutionalization process, specifically the closure of the North Princeton Developmental Center in New Jersey. Pre-existing data sets that utilized The New Jersey Division of Developmental Disabilities Client Assessment Form, Medical Assessment Form, and North Princeton Information Form and reflected information collected during the period 1994 through 1999 were used to obtain information for a specific group of movers (N = 87) and matched stayers (N = 87) at a baseline period, 3 months after, and 15 months after movers left the facility. A series of logistic regression analyses were conducted at the time intervals baseline to +3 months and +3 months to +15 months. Consistencies in findings over the two intervals were that higher cognitive levels were associated with higher incidence of challenging behavior, and greater mobility was associated with greater use of anti-psychotic medications. Residential environment was a factor in predicting medication use. The institutional group was more likely to receive psychotropic medication and less likely to be treated with alternate interventions than the community group. Results suggest that environmental influences had a greater impact on the incidence of challenging behavior to others and the use of psychotropic medications than intra-personal factors. This supports continued emphasis on the development of community-integrated environments and appropriate therapeutic milieus within these environments.
