AB  - This qualitative participatory action research addressed how interagency collaborations can improve services for African American mothers with child welfare cases and diagnosed with substance abuse and mental health disorders. The overarching research question was, 'How do substance abuse mental health treatment and child welfare workers describe their interagency collaborations for rural African American mothers, and what do they identify and recommend for service delivery and best practices for the identified stakeholders?' Participants were child welfare social workers and substance abuse mental health counselors who had worked with African American mothers and their families. Data were collected from three data sources to address triangulation: individual interviews, a focus group, and a research journal. Thematic data analysis showed three major themes: the effectiveness of collaboration efforts, barriers to services, and suggestions for improvement. These themes related back to the cyclical influence of the child welfare and substance abuse mental health systems in contributing to effective outcomes. The action plan included (a) recommendations to improve interagency collaborations, (b) a list of strategies to improve interagency communication, and (c) a plan to include the researcher to help implement the plan. Future researchers might explore this topic from the perspective of families to determine the variance in thoughts about areas of need and focus. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
