AB  - Compared presentation and treatment response between 2 groups of psychotic elderly patients both with and without dementia. Of 206 consecutive admissions to an inpatient geropsychiatry unit, 35 Ss (aged 63–86 yrs) with psychotic symptoms participated in the study. 13 of these had dementia and 22 had a primary Axis I psychotic disorder without dementia. Ss were treated with mild and high potency neuroleptics including loxapine and haloperidol (HAL); the mean HAL equivalents were compared between the groups. Ss with dementia receive lower dosages of medication to treat their symptoms. Both groups presented similarly, improved significantly from admission to discharge and demonstrated equivalent medication side-effect profiles. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
