AB  - The primary purpose of this study was to determine the impact of home health care services on quality of life of stroke survivors after discharge from hospital in Taiwan. The specific aims were (1) to determine whether there was a significant difference in quality of life of elderly stroke survivors who received home health care services and those who did not; (2) to determine whether there was a significant difference in functional ability, depressive symptoms, and perceived control between those who received home health care services and those who did not. This study used a pre-posttest quasi-experimental design with a comparison group. One hundred fifty-four post-stroke elderly were referred upon discharge by four hospitals and one home care agency in southwest Taiwan during a 17-month period. All of those referred were screened against the entry criteria: being 55 years of age or over, residing in home settings, having a degree of activity as Level 3 or 4 based on the Modified Karnofsky Scale, having cognitive functioning as evidenced through the use of the Short Portable Mental Status Questionnaire, having no aphasia, having no other non-stroke neuromuscular-skeletal physical disability, and having no psychiatric history. Each subject was measured twice with respect to quality of life, functional ability, depressive symptoms and perceived control, once within 10 days after home discharge and 3 months later. Initially 83 eligible subjects signed consent forms and completed Time 1 measures, 27 subjects withdrew sometime during a 3-month interval. Finally 29 remained in the intervention group and 27 remained in the comparison group; attrition rates were close to one-third. Most subjects in the two groups at Time 1 were totally dependent, moderately depressed and perceived a low degree of control over their lives and had a middle-low quality of life. Using an ANCOVA, the findings supported none of the hypotheses pertaining to the two aims of the study. Current home health care programs in Taiwan did not have more impact on the post-stroke elderly person's quality of life, functional ability, depressive symptoms and perceived control. The findings have implications for restructuring home health care services in Taiwan to promote the client's quality of life.
