AB  - The utilisation of opioids in patients with persistent pain in primary care is challenging. Several research groups in the United States have developed electronic pain diaries and prescription opioid monitoring software to improve patient care and pain related outcomes with the aim of reducing the incidence of opioid abuse. In 2014, a research team at the University of Nottingham developed a prototype smartphone application (app) to collect daily pain related outcomes for improving communication between patients and prescribers to promote safe and effective opioid prescribing. However, as longterm pain conditions are highly prevalent in the elderly population, the utility of the electronic diary and the willingness to access a smartphone app need to be justified and supported by patient views. This patient and public involvement (PPI) event aimed to elicit the opinions of patients and the general public with persistent pain, and their user experiences, to support the development of a smartphone app. This PPI event was held in Nottingham in November 2014. Patients with persistent pain conditions and members of the public were invited to participate in a focus group organised by the Nottingham Chronic Fatigue Syndrome and Fibromyalgia support group and researcher personal contact. This event was one of a series of stakeholder events which did not require ethical review. The focus group was facilitated by a researcher using a pre-designed interview schedule. Patients were asked about how they manage pain and the role of opioid medicines. All participants were asked to express their views regarding a potential smartphone App, and had an opportunity to test a pre-designed prototype on an Android platform and provide their feedback. Two researchers observed and took notes of the discussions and participants" approaches and responses when using the App. The notes were then analysed thematically. Four patients with persistent pain and one older person attended the event. Patients accepted that pain affected various aspects of their life (e.g. emotions, physical functioning, and relationships with other people) and recognised opioids were not a miracle cure. Patients were concerned about side effects, dependence and addiction of opioids, and used the lowest dose for the shortest duration. Participants used smart-phones on a daily basis and had no problems in using the prototype app. Patients were very positive about the potential of the prototype App and would like to track their progress over time. In addition, the functions of recording medication, alerting dosing time, ordering repeat prescriptions and recording adverse effects were considered essential and helpful. The results of this focus group support the concept that a smartphone App may help patients to improve their selfmanagement of pain. Patents valued the ability to make daily records of pain related outcome and would like to use the App to support their medication taking. However, the results were limited due to a small number of patients. Further research is needed to explore the use of the App in a wider group of patients with persistent pain.
