AB  - Discusses how current social policy affects clinical social work with children, adolescents, and their families, focusing on how trends such as the traditional inattention paid to children's rights and concerns, poverty, and the use of short-term policies that do not anticipate the consequences for future adults have left the basic needs of many children unmet. As a result of inadequate funding, many resources needed for prevention and therapeutic work are not adequate for the needs of the client group. Gaps in resources affect the welfare of children and their families and often contribute to the causes of mental health problems. Other issues related to service systems and professional arrangements affect the identity and status of clinical social workers and interfere with optimal professional activities. Among these issues are the fact that much of the therapy provided in places where poor children are typically seen (e.g., community mental health centers) is done so by workers with little experience and/or advanced training and that modalities such as crisis intervention, brief therapy, behavior modification, or drugs are mandated because of expediency or limited resources rather than as the preferred means of intervention. Means of enhancing professionals' access to and training in children's services and of restructuring service delivery are outlined. (34 ref) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
