AB  - Screening and Brief Intervention (SBI) is a comprehensive, integrated public health approach to the delivery of early intervention and treatment services for adults, particularly the large proportion who are not yet dependent but at risk of developing these disorders. SBI is thought to be effective in reducing alcohol and drug use problems in health care settings, including emergency departments (EDs) and trauma centers. These settings are thought to offer a potential "teachable moment" because patients may have perceptions of vulnerability about their health, and therefore be particularly receptive to screening and counseling. Although the SBI approach is a promising, potentially low-cost approach, few evaluation studies of the strategy are published. We present preliminary data on pre-post changes in alcohol and drug involvement in a large group of ED/trauma patients participating in CASBIRT, California's SBI program. CASBIRT has completed 100,000 screenings/interventions in 12 ED/trauma units over the past 2.5 years, providing services to a multi-ethnic patient population. A hallmark feature of CASBIRT is the delivery of services by non-medical bilingual (English/Spanish) paraprofessional health educators. Upon quickly determining the severity of use, an appropriate level of intervention is delivered. Most patients receive brief intervention immediately onsite in the ED that consists of client-centered feedback and motivational discussion. Follow-up telephone surveys were conducted with a random sample of 600 patients 6 months after patients received screening and brief intervention services. Pastmonth self-reported alcohol and drug abstinence rates more than doubled from intake (20%) to follow-up (44%) (p <=.05). Additional self-reported outcomes improved as well. Results of this SBI program evaluation indicate wide reach in terms of the numbers screened and in the diversity of those screened, as well as a high degree of acceptability among patients. Although no biological confirmation of abstinence at the follow-up was used and no control group was possible, self-reported pre-to-post program changes are promising. Screening and brief intervention delivered in acute care settings may provide an effective "teachable moment" that can motivate individuals to reduce alcohol and drug involvement.
