AB  - OBJECTIVE: As is characteristic of most chronic health conditions, heightened emotional distress is often comorbid with infertility. Despite this distress, few women experiencing infertility seek out mental health services. For this population, fears of being dismissed from reproductive treatment and stigmatization have been identified as barriers to seeking psychotherapy. There is a need for alternative modes of evidence-based treatment to support this population. The current study translated an in-person mind/body program for fertility into an online intervention to determine if participants were (1) willing to be recruited and randomized; (2) accepting and ready to engage in the intervention; and (3) less distressed over the course of the intervention. DESIGN: This pilot project was a randomized controlled feasibility trial using a between groups repeated measures experimental design. MATERIALS AND METHODS: Seventy-one women were recruited and randomized to the intervention or wait-list control group. Primary outcomes, included acceptance to engage in the intervention (i.e., retention), adherence (i.e., completing at least five out of ten modules), and satisfaction with intervention. Secondary outcomes sought to explore distress as measured by the Beck Anxiety Inventory (BAI), Beck Depression Inventory (BDI), Perceived Stress Scale, and Fertility Problem Inventory (variables collected at pre-, mid-, and post-assessment). This is an update to the mid-assessment data previously reported at ASRM with additional outcomes. RESULT(S): The retention, adherence, and satisfaction rates were similar to those reported in other internet-based studies. At post-assessment, relative to the wait-list group, the intervention group had statistically and clinically significant reductions in anxiety severity, beta = -4.86, 95%CI [-8.02, -1.70], depressive symptoms, beta = -7.98, 95%CI [-13.77, -3.13], and overall perceived stress, beta= -.29, 95%CI [-.57, -.01, as well as social, b = -4.32, 95%CI [-8.49, -.15], and sexual, beta = -6.39, 95%CI [-11.13, -1.66] concerns related to fertility. Effect sizes ranged from medium to large favoring the intervention. The odds of becoming pregnant was 3.08 higher for participants in the intervention group as compared to the wait-list group [CI 1.02, 9.29]. CONCLUSION(S): Results suggest that an online intervention for women experiencing infertility is both feasible and acceptable. Furthermore, the findings suggest that this intervention can reduce a broad range of distress in this population.
