AB  - Constipation is common among people with severe learning disabilities but its effect on their wellbeing is widely underestimated. This article provides a literature review of the causes and effects of constipation among this client group, and how the condition is managed. It suggests that many carers lack understanding of the effects of constipation on the wellbeing of people with severe learning disabilities and do not always make the appropriate interventions, including changes to clients’ diets and lifestyles. The article also suggests that community learning disability nurses are responsible for ensuring care staff have the information and training they need to provide good quality care.
