AB  - Describes a culturally sensitive approach (CSA) to cognitive–behavioral (CB) case formulation and a case illustration involving a Central American woman. CB models of major depression (MD) and panic disorder (PD) are reviewed including supporting empirical evidence. A CSA to treatment, with a brief overview of the Central American experience and strategies for outreach to Latinos based on their mental health and medical service utilization patterns, is presented. The case illustration describes a 27-yr-old woman from Nicaragua who relocated to the US. The client was referred to therapy for panic attacks that she had feared were heart attacks and presented with very sad, blunted affect and occasional tearfulness throughout evaluation. The client met criteria for MD and PD with subthreshold agoraphobia. The client's overt problems were viewed as resulting from the interaction of her highly stressful life events (divorce and relocation to the US) and dysfunctional thinking (involving the belief that she could no longer manage her life without her husband and was vulnerable to having a heart attack). The client completed 16 wks of group therapy, and her depression decreased from sever to moderate. The client was also able to reduce panic attacks and avoid full syndrome agoraphobia. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
