AB  - Capitation is a form of managed care that shifts financial risk for care onto providers. The objective is to create incentives for providers to contain costs and provide care in a more efficient manner. Critics of managed care for behavioral health (mental health and substance abuse services) are concerned that such attempts to contain costs would indiscriminately reduce all services as providers become more concerned about budgets. This dissertation tested the theory that capitation had an effect on children's outpatient care in a statewide behavioral health managed care program which had been shown to reduce inpatient services. The study analyzed administrative data for Medicaid funded mental health services provided by 17 Community Mental Health Centers (CMHCs). The focus of the study was the effects of capitation on the duration of outpatient episodes of care for young children ages 0-11, and the various factors that impacted the length of an outpatient episode for this age group within a system undergoing dramatic reform. It was hypothesized that this managed care plan, which effectively decreased inpatient admissions and costs for children, would result in longer outpatient episodes in line with the system of care philosophy of substituting alternate levels of care such as outpatient. It was also hypothesized that capitation would result in increased episode duration for children at clinical risk, as indicated by a disruptive behavioral diagnosis or having been hospitalized, two predictors of high service utilization. It was predicted that the capitated CMHCs would intervene with these vulnerable children by substitute outpatient care to avoid inpatient admissions. The study population included 100% of all outpatient service users, divided between two capitated organizational structures ('Direct Capitation' and 'Managed Behavioral Health Organization') and a group of CMHCs that remained fee for service. Direct Capitation (DC) was composed of four regional Mental Health Assessment and Service Agencies (MHASAs) consisting of six CMHCs that had contracts with the State and provided both the administrative risk management services and the direct delivery of services. Three of the non-profit CMHCs were in a consortium arrangement while the others were free standing. The Managed Behavioral Health Organization (MBHO) consisted of three MHASAs with eight CMHCs that formed the Colorado Health Network (CHN). The three MHASAs were separate joint ventures between non-profit CMHCs that delivered most of the services and a for-profit managed care company that provided the administrative risk management services. Data from claims and Medicaid encounter files were merged into episodes of care, defined as all services provided between 90-day gap periods. Cox proportional hazards regressions were run to estimate the hazard rate of ending treatment. The results supported the hypothesis that capitation resulted in an increase of outpatient episode duration, over and above similar effects for the comparison fee for services episodes. The hypothesis that capitation would result in longer outpatient episodes for children with disruptive behavioral diagnoses was supported, as capitation reversed the general trend of those episodes to be shorter. However, the hypothesis that similar capitation effects occurred for children having been hospitalized could not be supported, as capitation added no further effects over and above the trend in all organizational types to increase those children's outpatient episodes over time. (Abstract shortened by UMI.) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
