AB  - BACKGROUND: Weight gain, obesity, and resultant medical problems are increasingly recognized as critical medication side‐effects and pervasive problems in people with serious, persistent mental illness. While significant advances have been made in the pharmacological management of psychosis, the most common, serious medication side‐effect has become weight gain and related diabetes, hyperlipidemia, and hypertension. Multiple controlled trials support the efficacy of psychosocial weight loss interventions in this population, and these interventions are being included in upcoming national treatment guidelines. However, studies examining the implementation and effectiveness of these interventions in routine practice are lacking. EQUIP (“Enhancing QUality of care In Psychosis”), was a clinician‐level, randomized controlled trial of implementation of evidence‐based practices for schizophrenia. An objective of EQUIP was to evaluate the implementation and effectiveness of a weight program for individuals with schizophrenia at mental health clinics. METHODS: The study was conducted with 146 adults with schizophrenia who were randomly sampled from two mental health clinics of the Department of Veterans Affairs. Weight and BMI were calculated at baseline and approximately 15 months later. At each site, a clinician was trained to deliver a 16‐week, group‐based, psychosocial weight management program. Individuals who were overweight or obese (n=109) were offered this program and were weighed at each session. Clinicians were educated and prompted to refer eligible clients. RESULTS: Seventy‐five percent of clients at these clinics were overweight or obese. At baseline, awareness of weight loss interventions among clinicians was low, and these clinics offered no weight management services. After the weight management program was implemented, 51% of eligible clients chose to enroll. Those who enrolled were more likely to be obese (BMI>30), whereas those who did not enroll were more likely to be overweight (BMI of 25‐30; χ2 = 4.0, p < .05). Participants attended an average of 3.8 out of the 16 available group sessions. Participation in the weight program resulted in an average weight loss of 2.4 pounds and a BMI decrease of 0.3 units. There was no significant difference in weight or BMI change compared to the control group. CONCLUSIONS: There is a pressing need for interventions that reduce weight in people with serious, persistent mental illness. Implementation of an evidence‐based, psychosocial, weight management program is possible. However, in this study, treatment utilization was low. Although many clients expressed some interest in weight services and clinicians wanted a service they could refer to, delivery of the weight intervention proved to be quite challenging. Clients were often ambivalent regarding their need for weight loss, reluctant to participate in a group‐based intervention, and had limited transportation options. Adherence to and effectiveness of the intervention were lower than in prior efficacy research that was conducted with motivated, self‐selected individuals. To yield greater weight loss, intervention strategies should be developed that improve client motivation and utilization. The use of motivational interviewing and web‐based weight programs are being developed and will be presented.
