AB  - Objective: Patients with severe mental illness (SMI) have a reduced life-expectancy of 15-20 years. Metabolic screening is an effective method for early detection of treatable risk cardiovascular factors. Studies of metabolic screening report the prevalences of abnormalities found, but not participation rates of the intended study population. As timely detection of treatable cardiovascular risk factors in outpatients with SMI can contribute to a normalization of life expectancy in this high risk population, we decided for metabolic screening in all SMI outpatients who receive flexible assertive community treatment (Dutch version of ACT). Method(s): All patients in FACT-teams of one Dutch mental health organization (North-Holland North) were eligible for and asked to participate in metabolic screening. Screening was done by a group of three nurses that were specifically trained in metabolic screening. Result(s): In the study period (2012-2016) 58.5% of the total of 2061 patients participated in at least one metabolic screening. Participation was lower (56.3%) in outpatients and higher in sheltered living: 71.4%. Participation was lower in younger patients: 44.4% of the patients <30 years participated versus 59.4% of the patients >30 years. Sexe, drug addiction or psychiatric diagnosis didn't affect participation rates. The prevalence of the metabolic syndrome was 40.0%. Smoking was present in 65.9%. Alcohol addiction was present in 12.0%; social drinking in 40%. No gender differences were found. Substance abuse was present in 11.84% of the population. Abuse was more prevalent in men. Conclusion(s): Despite maximal effort, with specially trained and designated personnel, only 58% of the SMI-outpatients participated in metabolic screening. In the absence of registration of refusal, nonparticipating could not be analyzed. In the screened population, the metabolic syndrome was present in 40% and smoking in 65.9%.
