AB  - Tested the hypothesis that treatment with risperidone reduces readmission rates and associated costs when compared with treatment with perphenazine or haloperidol. Inpatients prescribed either risperidone, perphenazine, or haloperidol as a single oral antipsychotic at discharge were retrospectively identified. Data were collected for that index hospitalization and for a 1-yr follow-up period. Primary outcome measures included readmission rates, changes in antipsychotic therapy, anticholinergic drug use, and costs. There were 202 evaluable patients (81 treated with risperidone, 78 with perphenazine, and 43 with haloperidol). Baseline demographics were similar between groups except that more patients in the risperidone group had a primary diagnosis of psychotic disorder or had been hospitalized in the year prior to study. The percentage of patients readmitted during the 1-yr follow-up period was similar among drug groups when controlled for baseline differences in diagnosis and hospitalization history. Anticholinergic drug use was more common in the haloperidol group. Mean yearly cost (drug + hospitalization) in the risperidone group was $20,317, nearly double that in the other treatment groups. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
