AB  - A wide range of alcohol-related problems & offenses in the UK are examined, along with the difficulties faced by the courts in trying to deal with them. Available options have been limited by legislative restrictions, & attempts to expand provisions for drunkenness offenders have remained stalled. The contribution of various diversion schemes is considered, & illustrated via a case study of one operating in Scotland's Dumbarton District. Offenders considered suitable receive deferred sentences from the court & are assigned to an individual & group counseling service run by trained volunteers; social facilities are also provided. Short-term (6-12 months) & long-term (2 years) follow-up data on 48 alcohol-related offenders indicate that the program has been successful in preventing additional offenses; more research is necessary to determine whether other variables such as age, sex, marital status, employment, & previous patterns of drinking & offending are of any influence. The Dumbarton scheme demonstrates the utility of community-based intervention efforts & successful communication & operation between local voluntary organizations & the courts. 54 References. Modified HA
