AB  - Assessed the utility of community meeting participation as an indicator of treatment progress on a 5-day crisis stabilization psychiatric unit. Based on an extensive chart review of 70 patients' community meeting involvement ratings, active participation was associated with patient discharges to home vs transfer to extended treatment, and was correlated negatively with symptom severity. The factors most strongly associated with discharge were cooperativeness and appropriateness. Psychotic patients were less attentive and alert then those with mood and substance abuse disorders. Based on MMPI results obtained from 48 Ss, individuals exhibiting acute distress, neurotic functioning, and acting out behavior showed more community meeting improvement. (Dutch abstract) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2017 APA, all rights reserved)
