AB  - Examines the background, development, & content of a manualized home-based family intervention for ethnic minority families with a seriously mentally ill member, implemented through an Integrated Services Agency (ISA) in south-central Los Angeles, CA. Based on the premise that the client's home can be one of the most effective venues for achieving long-term positive outcomes from mental health services, the client is observed in the context of the home environment so that staff can more accurately assess the family dynamics & encourage family members to fulfill more effective roles as caregivers. Through effective case management & other interventions, the ISA has been able to change the service utilization pattern from inpatient care, homelessness, & incarceration to community treatment within the framework of integrated comprehensive services. Ten salient points that have been significant to this agency's positive outcomes are discussed, & a model is provided for conceptualizing the strengths of the study population -- low-socioeconomic status African Americans & Latinos -- in terms of the extended family, the kinship network, the church/spirituality, & strong education & work ethics (strengths), as well as the burdens -- racism, discrimination, & poverty in a poor inner-city community. Aside from semiweekly home visits by case managers, intervention includes family groups & family respite. 1 Figure, 52 References. Adapted from the source document.
