AB  - Mental illness is common among older adults who reside in the community. Few utilize specialty mental health care, however, due to barriers at the patient (e.g., cost, stigma), provider (e.g., attitudes, knowledge deficits), and systems levels (access, cost). Approaches to detection and treatment of older adults with depression and other mental illnesses have been developed for primary care practice settings as one means of surmounting these barriers. Another setting from which older adults at risk for mental illness commonly seek help is the aging services network (ASN). This session will examine the potential contributions of ASN agencies to the delivery of mental health care to seniors. The ASN is a national system of approximately 30,000 local and state aging services agencies that provide human services to over 10 million older adults and hundreds of thousands of care-givers annually. Services range from providing information and referral to home delivered meals, financial counseling, and intensive care management. Many older adults clients likely engage the ASN during periods of acute duress; others require social service referrals as a result of their mental conditions. Aging services professionals are therefore well positioned to interrupt a cycle of worsening emotional distress among this select group of vulnerable community-dwelling older adults. Panelists will provide an overview and specific examples of late life mental health care delivery in the ASN, emphasizing the challenges posed in their design and implementation by the "cultural" differences between health and human services settings. First, Dr. Conwell will review the structure and functions of the ASN and the available data on the prevalence of mental disorder among the clients it serves. Dr. Snowden will discuss the evidence base and issues encountered in implementation and dissemination of the Program to Encourage Active, Rewarding Livers for Seniors (PEARLS). PEARLS is an ASN-based treatment program designed to reduce depression in physically impaired and socially isolated older adults. Incorporating problem solving therapy, patient activation, and pleasant events scheduling, PEARLS is delivered by agency social work care managers with mental health consultation. Third, Dr. Sirey will present the Open Door study intervention. Built on the Treatment Initiation and Participation (TIP) model, the Open Door counselor provides depressed ASN clients education, identifies barriers to mental health care, and problem-solves to overcome those barriers. To conclude, interactive audience discussion lead by Dr. Conwell will consider the potential offered by incorporating ASN agencies into a comprehensive system of mental health care for older adults.
