T1  - The use of psychoeducation and mindfulness to mitigate the negative impacts of trauma exposure and increase overall well-being of direct contact staff in mental health facilities
T2  - Dissertation Abstracts International: Section B: The Sciences and Engineering
A1  - Cooper-Richardson Jillian N
KW  - eppi-reviewer
KW  - psychoeducation
KW  - mindfulness
KW  - mental health facilities
KW  - Psychoeducation
KW  - Mindfulness
KW  - Mental Health
PY  - 2017
DA  - 2017///
Y1  - 2017///
AB  - Numerous studies have demonstrated a consistent presence of trauma exposure in mental health direct contact staff (Adams, Boscarino, & Figley, 2006; Adams & Riggs, 2008; Bober, Regehr, & Zhou, 2006; Bride, 2007; Elwood, Mott, Lohr, & Galovski, 2011); showing prevalence rates as low as 7.1% and as high as 46.7% (Bober et al., 2006). Recent research has demonstrated the need for intervention in order to mediate the negative impact of trauma and the symptoms associated with trauma exposure (Bober & Regehr, 2005; Bride & Figley, 2009; Geller, Madsen, & Ohrenstein, 2004; Hesse, 2002); however, the field has yet to provide systematic preventative intervention. This study examined the negative impacts of trauma exposure on direct contact staff, resulting in trauma related symptomology, specifically an increase in anxiety and depression, and a decrease in overall well-being. It also explored the mitigating role of psychoeducation and mindfulness based intervention in reducing the negative impacts of trauma exposure, by presenting and utilizing an eight-session pilot program, Psychoeducation and Mindfulness Based Symptom Reduction Program (Rolens & Womontree, 2008). The program utilized handouts, activities, group process, and discussions to meet the needs of the direct contact staff who were routinely exposed to trauma. The session interventions were intended to: 1) reduce the negative impacts of trauma exposure, specifically the occurrence of depression and/or anxiety symptomology, 2) develop attentional control and awareness of senses, thoughts, and feelings within the moment, 3) utilize psychoeducation and mindfulness to understand individual reactions to trauma exposure and stress reactions, and 4) utilize psychoeducation and mindfulness to develop and apply healthy coping strategies to manage stressful situations and trauma exposure. This study utilized participant self-report to assess three hypotheses: 1) mental health direct contact staff (i.e., technicians, nurses, social workers, psychologists, psychiatrists, and administrators) will experience trauma related symptomology, specifically, elevated levels of depression and anxiety and decreased overall well-being, as a result of the demands of their occupation; 2) mental health direct contact staff will endorse a significant reduction in anxiety and depression, as well as a significant increase in overall well-being, after completing the pilot program, and 3) mental health direct contact staff will experience differing levels of trauma related symptomology dependent on their demographic characteristics (i.e., gender, education, occupation, partnership status, years in position, years in field, and direct contact hours). Data was collected over the course of 2 years, from 2008 to 2010, in a state run facility in a large urban area. The twenty-one participants were not required to have a clinical diagnosis of secondary trauma and ranged in age from 21 to 60, with 16 individual identifying as female and 5 as male. Analysis revealed that 12 participants reported clinical symptoms of anxiety (mild = 14.3%, moderate = 33.3%, and severe = 9.5%), 15 reported clinical symptoms of depression (mild = 9.5%, moderate = 42.9%, and severe = 19.0%), and 11 reported decrease in overall well-being (significant decrease = 33.3% and moderate decrease = 19.0%), providing support for the first hypothesis. The second hypothesis was also supported, as results of paired t-tests endorsed a significant decrease in participant anxiety, t(20) = 3.856, p < .01, and depression, t(20) = 7.947, p < .01, and a significant increase in overall well-being, t(20) = -4.740, p < .01, after completion of the psychoeducation and mindfulness based program intervention. One-way ANOVAs did not yield any support for the final hypothesis, demonstrating that there was no marked difference in anxiety, depression, and overall… (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2017 APA, all rights reserved)
