AB  - Professionals working in rehabilitation settings are exposed to specific distress. Many and various are the emotions elicited by working closely with psychotic clients and the intensity of these emotions are often difficult to cope with. Frustration and acting-out are very common and unpredictable, as are confusion of needs, fears and efforts to escape. A special psychodynamic, low cost supervision group has been established to aid professionals in the recognition and utilisation of their emotions in order to prevent stress and burnout. In this paper the approach is described and clinical examples are used to illustrate the method. Two main features of the supervision group are also stressed: the need for continuity and the potential positive effect on the emotional maturity of participants. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2017 APA, all rights reserved)
