AB  - Christine Costa California State University LONG BEACH, CALIFORNIA DOI: 10.1377/hlthaff.2016.0934 Providing Mental Health Care: The Author Replies Psychiatric nurse practitioners, mental health counselors, and several other professional groups in addition to psychiatrists, psychologists, and social workers clearly play important roles in the care of adults with serious mental illnesses, as Joel Miller and Christine Costa have noted. Yet despite the dedicated efforts of large numbers of skilled mental health professionals, approximately 40 percent of adults with serious mental illnesses in the United States- especially those who reside in remote and rural areas-received no treatment for their symptoms during the course of one year.1 While discussions of mental health treatment-seeking behavior have traditionally been framed in terms of patient predisposing, enabling, and need factors, critical shortages in the mental health workforce constitute easily overlooked and often important barriers. Some promising policy approaches include reorienting training programs toward the assessment and management of serious mental illnesses, expanding loan repayment programs to incentivize practice in underserved areas, developing collaborative care teams that offer specialized mental health care in general medical settings, and increasing reimbursement of evidence-based treatment for adults with serious mental illnesses, as noted in my June 2016 article.
