AB  - Background: Depression is a common mental illness worldwide which prevalence is estimated from 3% to 5% [1]. Additionally, it is ranked among the top five contributors to the global burden disease, and the global cost per year along with anxiety disorders is estimated in $1.15 trillion [2]. Functioning impairment is significantly prevalent and most patients prioritized functional over clinical outcomes [3]. Cognitive impairment is well established as core feature of depression with a strong neurof unctional association. Neuroimaging studies found a pattern of underactivated prefrontal cortex areas and overactivated subcortical areas [4]. Besides, some studies point out cognition could influence more than clinical symptoms in functional outcomes [5]. Therefore, our two aims are: (1) Study the relation between psychosocial functioning in depression with sociodemographic, clinical and neuropsychological variables, as well as analyze possible predictors of this functioning. (2) Study if cognition and/or psychosocial functioning can predict treatment adherence. Method(s): We recruited 22 participants (19 women, mean age 48.32 +/- 8.2, mean episodes 2.9 +/- 1.30, mean episode duration 15.36 +/- 14.81) with current Major Depression Episode following the Structured Clinical Interview-DSM IV (SCID-I) and derivated to Group Cognitive Behaviour Therapy (10 sessions) from Outpatient Mental Healthcare services of Terrassa Hospital. Hamilton Depression Rating Scale (HDRS; Hamilton 1967) and Beck Depression Inventory (BDI-II; Beck et al 1996) were used to determine the severity of the episode and neuropsychological test were administered to analyze cognitive performance. Digits forward/backward of Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS-III; Wechsler et al, 1996) to test attentional spam and working memory, Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (WCST-64; Heaton et al, 2000) to test abstract and flexible thinking, Stroop Test (Golden, 1978) to test inhibitory control and California Verbal Learning Test (CVLT; Delis et al 1987) to test verbal memory. WHO Disability Assessment Schedule (WHODAS 2, 36-item) assessed Psychosocial Functioning at treatment baseline. Spearman Correlation Coefficient and Regression Analyses were used as appropriate. Result(s): There are no relation between sociodemographic or severity symptoms and psychosocial functioning. Neither are with treatment adherence. WHODAS Cognition has negative relation with WCST perseverative errors (Rho:-0.048, p = 0.037) and positive with backward digit spam (Rho: 0.443, p = 0.044), Digits total (Rho: 0.558, p = 0.009) and Stroop PC (Rho: 0.445, p = 0.038). WHODAS number of days without activity has negative correlation with Stroop PC (Rho:-0.552, p = 0.042). Treatment adherence has negative correlations with WHODAS self-care (Rho:-0.673, p = 0.008), WHODAS reduction of activity days (Rho:-0.634, p = 0.015), suicide attempts (Rho:-0.577, p = 0.005) and WCST total errors (Rho:-0.459, p = 0.032). While Positive correlations with WCST number of perseverative responses (Rho: 0.435, p = 0.043). Regression analysis found that attention predicts WHODAS cognition (p = 0.029) and inhibitory control predicts WHODAS days of inactivity (p = 0.039). While suicide attempts (p = 0.013), WHODAS selfcare (p = 0.011) and WCST total errors (p = 0.045) predict treatment adherence. Conclusion(s): Cognitive performance impact in psychosocial functioning and psychological treatment adherence in our depression sample. Poor inhibitory control predicts more days of inactivity and worse psychosocial functioning, while executive functioning and suicide attempts at baseline predict worse adherence to psychological treatment. These results highlight the role of cognition in depression outcomes.
