AB  - Learning Objectives: The Joint Commission reports that communication breakdowns are responsible for 85% of sentinel events in hospitals. Transitions of care between providers are vulnerable periods in healthcare delivery that expose patients to preventable errors and adverse events. We developed a neurocritical care unit (NCCU) transition program to facilitate interdisciplinary communication and provide clinical support for patients with resolving critical illness. Method(s): All patients admitted to the NCCU longer than 24 hours are included. Prior to the patient transfer, a transfer note is completed by the NCCU provider, summarizing the hospital course and listing key issues for follow up. An in person sign out is provided to the receiving team, allowing for the opportunity to ask questions in real time. A NCCU Advanced Practice Clinician (APC) evaluates each patient 48-72 hours after transfer from the NCCU. The APC follow up includes reviewing the plan of care, reviewing goals of care with the patient and/or family, coordinating care with other members of the health care team, and facilitating escalations in care if indicated. Recommendations for changes in the plan of care are documented and communicated directly to the current provider. Result(s): Six months after initiation of the program, an electronic survey was distributed to physicians, residents, APCs and nurses in the neuroscience department. Seventy nine percent of respondents felt that the program has improved overall patient care. The majority of the respondents (86%) felt that the program has improved the continuity of care and that communication has improved (67%). As reported by the APCs, issues addressed during the post NCCU evaluation include pulmonary interventions, medication reconciliation, blood pressure and fluid management, patient and family communication, and delirium prevention. Conclusion(s): The implementation of a comprehensive transition program can facilitate communication during care transfers and improve overall patient care. Opportunities for improvement of the program include an evaluation for the ongoing need of invasive lines and indwelling catheters, and completion of formal cognitive screening during the post NCCU evaluation.
