AB  - This study investigates how the social support found in the job club approach to job search can act as a buffer to stress variables and increase job seeking behaviors. The following research questions were investigated: (1) At pre-test, what was the relationship of locus of control, self-esteem, and depression? (2) At post-test, what was the relationship between measures of social support and measures of self-esteem, depression, and locus of control? (3) From pre-test to post-test, did participation in a social support group affect social support measures? (4) From pre-test to post-test, did social support affect locus of control, depression and self-esteem? (5) At post-test, after members have been participating in a social support group, what effect did the participation have on job seeking behavior? (6) From pre-test to post-test, was job seeking behavior affected by length of unemployment? This quasi-experimental study involved an experimental group (N = 45) of members of a job club for unemployed professionals. The comparison group (N = 40) was solicited from a state employment development department job program and a non-profit, drop-in job resource center. Pre-test data was collected when the experimental group joined the job club and when the comparison group began to utilize the services of the job resource centers. Post-test data was collected after an average of six to eight weeks. Results indicated that attachment and reassurance of worth support were related to high self-esteem. Attachment support was also a buffer to depression. Participation in a social support group based on the job club concept was found to have a significant effect on job seeking behaviors. The job seeking behaviors of the experimental group increased from pre-test to post-test, while those of the comparison group decreased. Length of unemployment was not a predictor for job seeking behaviors. Implications of these findings and suggestions for further research are discussed. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
