AB  - This paper undertakes to trace the development of group work as it is being used for distinctly therapeutic purposes. Although it is always difficult to set a definite date for a beginning-since the past always runs into the present and the present gives promise of what is to come-this historical presentation will be considered in three periods. First will come the period in which group work services developed in various therapeutic settings, approximately the period of the last twenty years. Then one shall flashback by discussing the developments in group work immediately preceding this branching off into therapeutic settings. Finally, one shall return to the present in terms of what seem to be the crucial questions for the field. One knows that some experimentation in the use of groups appeared very early in some of the agencies that later developed group work. As scholar Gisela Konopka reports, Hull House had a group-apparently with some success-for youthful drug addicts in 1909 and also experimented with housing groups of the mentally ill.
