AB  - Several terms have been developed, including secondary traumatic stress (STS), to describe the effects of trauma work on those in helping professions. In order to help understand the cultural nuances of STS, the current research team utilized transcripts of qualitative focus groups of 26 participants in an organization serving families in the Guatemala City Dump. Members of focus groups discussed what they felt qualified as child sexual abuse. The transcripts were examined to determine whether these aid workers were exposed to trauma, if language of STS was evidenced, and if cultural nuances existed to explain how they might be affected by working with a high-risk population. Limitation of the study and training issues for work in similar communities are discussed. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
