AB  - [...]there is some evidence that physicians alter the stated diagnosis depending on the motivation for reporting the diagnosis. [...]insurance claims, medical records, and physicians' diaries may differ for the same patient.14 Our analysis suggests a strategy that health plans could use to manage depressed patients more efficiently. [...]although our exclusion of patients with psychosis and substance abuse disorders limits the generalizability of our results regarding mental illness, one might predict similar results in dually diagnosed patients with these conditions. [...]whereas new treatments appear to reduce the cost and improve the quality of depression care, there does not appear to be a similar reduction in costs for comorbid medical illnesses.16 Implications for cost-effectiveness research. Cost-effectiveness studies of depression care are usually based on extrapolations from clinical trials or otherwise limited patient populations.17 These studies are unlikely to capture the full range of depressed patients, especially those who are the highest cost. Because these costs appear to be related to comorbid conditions, cost-effectiveness studies based on more limited populations are likely to overstate potential cost savings and therefore overstate cost-effectiveness.
