AB  - A study concludes that, as it is now applied, the Americans with Disabilities Act only partially accommodates the needs of mentally depressed workers. On the positive side, it allows employers to actively encourage necessary treatment. Unfortunately, this only helps those who are willing to come forward in what appears to be a "don't ask, don't tell" environment. Furthermore, by failing to recognize the clinical course of depressive disorders, current interpretations may impede full return to normal functioning and effective relapse-prevention strategies. Future legislation and regulatory policies should encourage aggressive outreach that provides needed treatment while limiting the risk of subsequent discrimination.
