AB  - Compared the entrance and exit scores of 16 male sex offenders completing treatment at an outpatient program specializing in cognitive–behavioral treatment. The treatment program consisted of individual behavior therapy sessions, group therapy, and family therapy. Outcome measures included the MMPI, the Multiphasic Sexual Inventory (MSI), and recidivism (rearrest record) posttreatment. Statistically significant improvements were obtained on a number of these measures including the 2 scales of the MMPI and Child Molest and Denial scales of the MSI. One of the 16 graduates reoffended during the average follow-up period of 26 mo. These data are supportive of the contention that outpatient behavior therapy can be effective in reducing deviant sexual arousal and in enhancing appropriate consensual sexual behavior. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
