AB  - The purpose of this study was to determine the characteristics of families who were successful in a Family Preservation Program (FPP) in Northeast Ohio. Seventy-five percent of the families were successful. Quantitative data was collected from case records of the 113 families. Two case studies were used to develop an in-depth look at one successful and one unsuccessful family in the program. Two focus groups were employed to explore the professional perspectives of family preservation therapists (n = 7) and community mental health workers (n = 4) about the characteristics of successful and unsuccessful families. Results indicated that the major characteristic of families with successful outcomes was having a good attitude toward working with the professionals to retain the child in the home. Other characteristics contributing to successful outcomes in the program included (a) involvement of the mother, (b) personality strengths of the parent(s), and (c) community support. The major characteristic of families who were unsuccessful in the program was an involvement of a family member with substance abuse. Other characteristics leading to unsuccessful outcomes were (a) a family member involved with the legal system, (b) unresolved mental health problems of a parent, (c) the child attending more than three schools, and (d) the child placed out of the home during the program. Results of this study suggest that the parents not only set the tone for the intervention process, but are thought to be a major focus of the intervention process. This finding merits further study, possibly comparing therapy with parents only versus, or in addition to, the family unit. Results of this study further suggest the importance of an early therapeutic relationship between therapist and family, and also suggested the need for family preservation therapists to acquire knowledge in and work closely with drug/alcohol therapists. These factors, plus efforts to stabilize the family within a support network of family and community, may improve FPP outcomes in the future. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
