Y1  - 2012///
AB  - There is no doubt that the global financial meltdown has had devastating effects on economies throughout the world, particularly those of less developed countries in general and Sub-Saharan Africa in particular. For instance, there is general consensus that the world economic and financial crisis has impacted negatively and significantly on direct foreign investment, reduced considerably commodity prices and further plunged Sub-Saharan African countries and their economies into trade and budget deficits of monumental proportions. In the aftermath of the current economic recession, the first in the Southern African Development Community (SADC) region in almost two decades, many countries and their economies are experiencing slow growth, a phenomenon that has inevitably resulted in astronomical job losses. Unemployment in Sub-Saharan Africa is arguably one of the most formidable challenge facing governments in this volatile part of the continent. In South Africa - a country that is the fulcrum of economic growth and development in Africa in general and SADC in particular - approximately 5.5 million people, which statistically translates to one in every six adults, are unemployed. In Zimbabwe, a country that once enjoyed the enviable reputation of being the breadbasket of economic development in Southern Africa, unemployment numbers a staggering 80%, while in Swaziland 40% of the population is unemployed. The significance of such high levels of unemployment, which are widespread throughout Sub-Saharan Africa, lies in the fact that unemployment is one of the most important causes of poverty, underdevelopment and inequality and, directly or indirectly, contributes to political instability. In this paper, after a brief examination of the causes and consequences of the global economic and financial meltdown, we discuss the challenges the crisis poses for countries in Sub-Saharan Africa, many of whose fragile economies have slipped further down the abyss of a world recession and a financial storm of historic proportions. In tracing the historical evolution, causes as well as consequences of the global economic crisis, which a majority of leading economists and experts regard as the most serious and devastating since the Great Depression of the 1930s, we observe that, precipitated by the 2008 subprime mortgage meltdown in the USA, its global effects are characterized by the collapse of key players in business, declines in consumer spending, significant financial commitments incurred by governments and a substantial slowdown in economic activity. Reprinted by permission of the African Training and Research Center in Administration for Development
