AB  - Background and Aims: Surgery for large kidney stones; i.e. Percutaneous Nephrolithotomy (PNL), is often associated with severe postoperative pain. Method(s): Approved by Regional Ethics Committee & Danish Medicine Agency. Registered at Clinical Trials NCT02818140.60 patients ASA 1-3 were enrolled in the study. All patients scheduled for elective PNL under general anaesthesia. Multimodal analgesic regime consisting of oral acetaminophen 1 g, dexamethasone 4 mg & 0.25 mg/kg sufentanil I.V. 30 minutes prior to emergence. Patients were allocated to TQL block with either ropivacaine 30 ml 0.75% or 30 ml saline. Primary Outcome:Morphine consumption 0-6 postoperative hours. Secondary Outcomes: Discharge time, accumulated opioid consumption 24 hours postoperatively, time to first opioid, ambulation time, length of stay (LOS). Morphine consumption recorded via Patient Controlled Analgesia (PCA) pump. Result(s): Oral morphine equivalents administered 0-6 hour mean +/-SD 7.5 (8.7) vs. 90.6 (69.9) mg, p<0.0001 was significantly reduced in the intervention group compared to control. A significant reduction in accumulated oral morphine equivalents in intervention group for 24 hours was recorded (Table 1). Time to first opioidmean +/-SD647.3 (402.7) vs. 39.1 (23.7) minutes, p<0.0001was significantly prolonged in intervention group (Figure 1), and intervention group was able to ambulate significantly earlier mean +/-SD 365.7 (254.5) vs. 636.2 (366.2) minutes, p<0.004 ) (Figure 2). LOS was significantly shorter in the intervention group mean (days) +/-SD 2.00 (0.78) vs 3.03 (1.25) p=0.001 compared to control Conclusion(s): Unilateral TQL block significantly reduces the postoperative opioid consumption and facilitates significantly earlier discharge from hospital. (Figure Presented).
