AB  - Research Objectives: To gather information from brain injury (BI) survivors about long-term health care needs, quality, barriers, and facilitators to accessing care in the community. Design(s): Qualitative content analysis of data gathered in focus groups using semi-structured interviews. Setting(s): Two community-based clinical research centers. Participant(s): 44 community-dwelling adults with BI: 58% female, 88% college educated, 76% currently unemployed, mean time post injury of 16 years, mean (range) age 53 (23-72) years. Participants were recruited through BI support groups and advisory groups at two research centers (one each on the east and west coasts of the United States). Intervention(s): N/A. Main Outcome Measure(s): Participants were asked open-ended questions about their experiences with health care in the community with regard to care needs, utilization, access, barriers, and facilitators to health management. Result(s): Qualitative content analysis identified central themes that emerged consistently across both study sites. Barriers to health care access/ utilization included BI-related cognitive and physical deficits, often exacerbated by external barriers (e.g., logistics, costs). Facilitators included certain qualities/skills of clinicians (e.g., knowledge of BI, sensitivity to BI-related deficits), assistance from caregivers, and access to specialty care even many years after injury. The importance of communication, both as a facilitator and barrier to care, was mentioned by most participants. Compensatory strategies and external tools were identified as key facilitators of medical self-management. Finally, improving primary care clinicians' knowledge about BI emerged as a potential solution to address health needs of individuals with chronic BI. Conclusion(s): The findings from this qualitative study suggest several specific, low-cost modifications to health care delivery and strategies for improving medical self-management that can maximize long-term health maintenance for BI survivors.
