AB  - Discusses the supportive group experience for patients with early-stage Alzheimer's disease (AD). It is observed that support groups are an invaluable ingredient in the treatment of unimpaired patients and caregivers, as well as caregivers of individuals with cognitive impairments. An early stage dementia support group: (1) provides a therapeutic intervention for a subset of AD patients who are vulnerable to states of depression, frustration, and crisis; (2) identifies specific issues unique to this population; (3) evaluates the influence of a psychotherapeutic group experience on patients' self-esteem, coping skills, and sense of well-being; and (4) develops practical recommendations from which a model for early-stage AD support groups may follow. Early participation by AD individuals in a support group might lead to better understanding and acceptance of the inevitable changes in function, role, and identity. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
