AB  - Health visitors and clinical medical officers were trained in parent counselling and worked at home in a highly deprived area with parents of preschool children with multiple psychosocial problems. Ss were 2 groups of families: 55 in the intervention group (IG) and 38 in the comparison group (CG). Ss were interviewed and completed self-report questionnaires assessing issues such as, maternal self-esteem, parental stress, maternal anxiety and depression, distress, familial relationships, social support, home environment and child behavior problems. The researchers followed up with the IG group 2–15 wks and the CG group 16.2 wks after initial assessment. Results show that the Service was highly valued by both parents and referrers, and suggested significant changes, including: improvements in the severity of problems; increased parental self esteem; decreased levels of parental stress and emotional difficulties, more positive constructions of their children; improvements in the home environment; and decreased child behavioral problems. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
