AB  - Assessed a special hospital-based treatment program for male mentally handicapped offenders, which consisted of practical and personal skills training coupled with a socialization program based on token-economy strategies within a controlled and structured environment. 20 patients (aged 16–36 yrs, IQ range 58–81) admitted to the program and followed up after discharge for an average of 3.3 yrs were studied. All had committed serious or persistent offenses and showed a high level of psychosocial pathology. A good or fair response to the treatment program was made by 85%, and 65% were judged well-adjusted or reasonably well-adjusted at last follow-up contact. A good outcome was associated with more than 2 yrs' inpatient care, a good response to the treatment program and stable residential placement, regular occupation, and regular supervision and support in the community. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2018 APA, all rights reserved)
