AB  - We systematically reviewed the studies investigating pharmacological interventions for pain among persons with opioid use disorder (OUD). Our results highlights early signals for drugs with GABAergic and glutamatergic mechanisms of action, which warrant further exploration. Our review also underscores the need for methodological improvements and broader treatment targets in future studies. Various biological systems play crucial roles in modulating pain- and addiction-related behavior-which are heavily influenced by multifaceted factors such as affect and cognitive state. A greater mechanistic understanding of these systems suggests promising future treatments for OUD and chronic pain. Whilst much has been achieved in the fields of pain and OUD within the past few decades, there is a pressing need for conceptual and methodological integration, so that the clinical potential of biological systems modulating pain and addictive behavior can be fully realized, improving patients' lives along both domains. Background(s): The clinical features and neurobiology of chronic pain and OUD are inextricably linked. Despite emerging evidence supporting the negative impact of chronic pain in the treatment of OUD, the pharmacological management of pain in the presence of OUD has received limited attention. We sought to systematically review the human studies investigating medications for pain in OUD. Objective(s): 1) Summarize the shared clinical features and neurobiological mechanisms of OUD and chronic pain. 2) Review the results of the human studies investigating pharmacological interventions for pain in OUD. 3) Describe future treatment targets for pain in OUD. Method(s): Eligible studies had participants with OUD and the study outcomes included evoked or spontaneous pain. We searched Scopus, Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews, Medline, and Embase. Result(s): Out of 1097 studies that met the search criteria, 12 studies provided data relevant to the research question-5 laboratory studies and 7 clinical trials. Random effects pooled estimates suggested no significant difference between groups at baseline, but a response favoring the active treatment group over placebo, with non-significant heterogeneity between studies. Findings from these studies provide preliminary evidence for analgesic and anti-hyperalgesic effects of gabapentin, GABA agonists, and NMDA receptor antagonists as potential analgesics in persons with OUD. Conclusion(s): Attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) pharmacotherapy is associated with increased long-term retention in outpatient addiction treatment. In view of the evidence for worsened outcomes among individuals with comorbid ADHD, judicious use of targeted pharmacotherapy may be an important component of treatment-planning. Future studies should address optimal timing of medication initiation relative to abstinence state, and also quantify risk of stimulant or nonstimulant therapy within the monitored treatment frame of addiction care. Scientific Significance: This review underscores novel treatment targets and the need for methodological enhancement in drug development for pain in OUD. The development of novel therapeutics for pain in OUD may require further conceptual and methodological integration of pain and addiction research.
