AB  - Antipsychotics have been the mainstay of treatment for schizo-phrenia for about 50 years. Current antipsychotics all essentially use the same mechanism of action, that is, blocking the postsynaptic DA2 receptors, similar to the drugs discovered in the 1950s [1]. Antipsychotics are used as the core feature in long-term management in schizophrenia and are effective in treating psychosis and reducing the risk of relapse but largely fail to treat other aspects of the disorder and have troubling side effects. A number of studies have shown non-adherence to be a frequent problem and a common cause of relapse, rehospitalization and loss of hard-won gains in psychosocial adjustment [2]. For this purpose, we arranged a study based on following up 30 patients treated with Aripiprazole depot along 18 months. In 17 patients the treatment with Abilify Maintena were prescribed in outpatient visits and in 13 patients after a psychotic relapse in Hospitalitation Unit. We took in account some variables as, treatment continuation among this period of time, satisfaction degree with it, number of relapses in between, psychopathological status and general functionality measured using PANSS and EuroQol Scales, and also metabolic affection secondary to antipsychotic drugs. Apart from that, we made a difference between patients in monotherap, what means they just took Aripiprazole, neither benzodiazepines nor other antipsychotics (6 patients of our sample) or in combination therapy (24 patients of our sample). Furthermore, we considered also some relevant aspects, such as, the severity of the clinical picture and the requirement of other Specialized Care Units: Active Rehabilitation Unit (URA), Therapeutic Community and Center of Drug Addiction Treatment and Rehabilitation, besides to the outpatients visits. In our sample, about 35% of the patients were receiving simultaneously assistance in some of these Specific Units, what speaks of the severity of the clinical prof ile. Therefore, we obtained that about 58% patients withhold the treatment with Aripiprazole depot, some of those cases require adding a second oral antipsychotic, about 15% change to oral Aripiprazole and about 37% of the patients had to change to another antipsychotic.Another important aspect are the frequence of relapses and the causes of it. These results could enlighten us to know better the functioning of this drug and determine the clinical prof ile, to whom it would be more accurate, thereby facilitating translation of the research into best practice.
