AB  - Examined the effectiveness of music therapy in short-term group psychotherapy for 8 adults with chronic mental illness (bi-polar disorder, depressed phase; schizophrenia, undifferentiated type or paranoid type, with and without history of substance abuse). The following 3 research questions were asked: (1) Will patients report a change in symptomatolgy?, (2) Will patients report a change in the presence of curvative factors?, and (3) Will patients report a change in attitude toward seeking help? Data for this study were collected via 3 different self-report measurement tools. Results show that group music therapy for adults with mental illness may help to reduce psychiatric symptoms related to anxiety. Also, patients perceive music therapy as helpful and improves attitudes toward help seeking and openness about one's problem. The authors suggest that additional research is needed to either confirm or dispute these findings, and to develop more stabilized music therapy techniques for use in mental health settings. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
