AB  - Stanford accepted only one out of seven potential candidates, and the NHTS adopted this figure. [...]in the patient screening and selection process envisioned by the NHTS, annual demand from some 85,000 Medicare beneficiaries who might benefit from a heart transplant dwindles to a mere 140 admitted as candidates. Stanford's is reportedly age fifty-nine, while Pittsburgh uses age sixty-one.16 Anecdotal information from several transplant centers leads me to believe that "official" age limits are routinely exceeded. Because the incidence of heart disease increases rapidly with age, widening the age range greatly expands potential demand for heart transplants. Several states observed declines in fatalities following introduction of compulsory seat belt legislation, and campaigns against drunken driving have also been mounted in recent years.18 However, these measures have had little effect in reducing motor vehicle mortality, and alcohol-related deaths may actually be on the rise.19 Furthermore, gains in automobile safety are being offset by trauma among users of motorcycles and threewheeled "all terrain" vehicles.20 While the size of the potential donor pool may be stable, the annual number of persons brain-dead in a hospital may have grown over the past few years as an unexpected outcome of improved emergency medical services programs. Because donor availability is the ultimate constraint on transplant volume, Exhibit 3 also gives the annual number of organ donors (based on kidney transplants) for the same period.
