AB  - Background and Purpose. Interventions based on the health belief model (HBM) and audiovisual health education strategies have successfully increased mammogram utilization; however few have been implemented among low-income Hispanic women. The purpose of this study was to examine whether targeted cancer prevention education based on the HBM influenced mammogram screening beliefs and increased intentions of low-income Hispanic women to obtain mammograms compared to a control group receiving no education. Method. This was a posttest-only control group experiment. We obtained qualitative and quantitative information to identify health beliefs and mammogram intentions of 210 low-income Hispanic women, ages 40 years and above. Two focus groups were conducted to examine perceptions related to breast cancer and mammogram screening. Randomization to either an intervention or a control group occurred by half-day of the week at two health clinics. The Revised Susceptibility, Benefits and Barriers Scale, the Seriousness Scale, and the Self-efficacy Scale for Mammography were used to measure health beliefs (Champion, 1993; 1999; Champion, Skinner, & Menon, 2005). Ajzen's (2006) recommendations for assessing intention were used to measure mammogram intentions. The NCI's Breast Cancer Risk Assessment Tool (2009) was used to measure breast cancer risk. Seven independent samples t-tests and two multiple linear regressions were performed using SPSS to compare differences between intervention and control groups on HBM variables and to predict mammogram intentions. Results and Conclusions. Participants in the intervention reported significantly greater perceived benefits (p < 0.05), benefits minus barriers (p < 0.05), self-efficacy (p < 0.01) and intention to obtain a mammogram ( M = 4.38, SD = 0.46; p < 0.001). There was a significant linear relationship between intention and predictor variables (p <0.001); study group, HBM variables, and covariates accounted for 53.4% of the variance in intention. After controlling for covariates, receiving the intervention (p < 0.001), and having greater perceived benefits (p < 0.01), decreased barriers ( p < 0.01), a greater net score of benefits minus barriers ( p < 0.001), and greater self-efficacy (p < 0.001) independently predicted mammogram intentions. These findings provide information about developing tailored health education strategies for low-income Hispanic women to achieve Healthy People 2010 objectives. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
