AB  - Purpose: Risk and prevalence of problematic alcohol and other substance use greatly increases over adolescence. Research that establishes efficacious alcohol screening and treatment for youth in communitymental health centers is imperative. Method(s): Community mental health providers were trained to screen youth for alcohol use, and then providers were randomly assigned to and trained in one of three treatment arms: (1) treatment as usual (TAU; based on cognitive behavioral therapy), (2)motivational interviewing (MI), or (3)MI with additional coaching (MI + coaching). Youth receiving treatment from trained providers were recruited (N = 209; 9-18 years old; 57%female; 23% Hispanic; 75%White, 13%Black, 7%American Indian) and assessed on number of drinking and binge drinking days, number of marijuana use days, and number of cigarette use days using Timeline Followback at baseline, 3- and 6-months post-baseline. Generalized estimating equations (GEE) were used tomodel change in alcohol and other substance use over time by treatment group. Baseline substance use, gender, and age were entered as covariates in the models. GEE allows modeling for non-normally distributed outcomes (e.g., count data), and thus negative binomial regressions were used for all outcomes. Result(s): Findings indicated that youth in the MI and MI + Coaching groups had significantly lower rates of cigarette smoking across follow-up assessments compared to the TAU group (IRR [Incidence Rate Ratio] = 0.24, p = 0.04; and IRR = 0.21, p = 0.02, respectively). Regardless of treatment group, youth were found to decrease frequency of binge drinking from 3- to 6-months, although this effect did not reach statistical significance (IRR = 0.43, p = 0.16). No other significant interaction or treatment effects were found. Conclusion(s): Although all three conditions reduced alcohol use over time, MI was more effective in reducing cigarette smoking whether providers were coached or not. In conclusion, screening and evidenced- based treatments in community settings that work with at-risk youth may be useful in addressing and preventing risky substance use.
