AB  - Background/Purpose: This presentation will discuss efforts to implement meaning-centered psychotherapy (MCP) for advanced cancer patients in a mental health treatment center for World Trade Center rescue, recovery, and cleanup workers (WTC responders). Method(s): Treatment services provided by the federally funded World Trade Center Health Program are available at no cost for WTC responders with qualifying health conditions associated with their response efforts. As a group, WTC responders experience a growing burden of physical and mental health conditions, including over 70 types of cancer that have been associated with WTC toxic exposure. Increased cancer incidence together with mental health comorbidity in WTC responders presented a pressing treatment need for which there was a gap in evidence-based services available. Result(s): The Mount Sinai World Trade Center (WTC) Mental Health Program has collaborated with Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center to address the unique mental health needs of WTC responders. Twenty-eight Mt. Sinai WTC Program clinicians have been trained in Meaning Centered Psychotherapy, an evidence-based seven to eight session individual or group intervention targeting hopelessness and loss of meaning in life-limiting illness. Alternative treatments were considered, but MCP had the most compelling evidence-based and a robust training and supervision process. MCP was also chosen because it targets existential issues reported by responders, including how role changes (eg, no longer being able to work due to illness) negatively impact sense of purpose. The presentation will address how MCP has been modified for WTC responders, and will feature several case examples. Themes include how the events of September 11, 2001 and resulting illnesses make it difficult to connect with sources of meaning. Conclusions and Implications: The positive patient response to MCP interventions has been encouraging. MCP has increased interest in further psychotherapy and reduced hopelessness. Further directions, including systematizing adaptations and increasing dissemination of MCP for this population, will be discussed.
