AB  - Discusses the etiology, psychological implications, and a therapeutic intervention for women affected by narcissistic disturbances due to deprived mother–daughter relationships during early life. The syndrome occurs when, during the period of early infancy in which the infant perceives itself as the center of the universe, the mother perceives the infant as an extension of herself. As a result, the infant does not develop a sense of emerging, deserved separateness. The treatment described, and illustrated via a case study, combines individual psychotherapy with a female therapist and group therapy in an all-female group. The group gives freer reign to transferential connections with mother figures, expediting some of the resolution of the early problem. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
