AB  - In three studies, we investigated the effect of diagnosis (i.e., depression versus a physical illness) on symptom perception and social support. Study 1 compared perceptions of objectively equivalent depressive symptoms labeled as either 'depression' or 'a heart condition.' Participants who imagined they were experiencing 'depression' reported that their symptoms were due more so to psychological causes generally subject to internal control, and they considered social support as more important for improvement. In Studies 2 and 3, participants were told that another person was experiencing depressive symptoms reflecting a diagnosis of either 'depression' or 'a thyroid condition.' For these studies, the perceived reward of interacting with the other person played a critical role in moderating the influence of diagnosis, with low perceived reward being associated with a minimization of the depressed other's need for social support. We discuss the implications of these findings for understanding the stigmatization and amelioration of depression. Reprinted by permission of Guilford Press
